{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3xprxrjpbvaxcl76z3zylcq6ochc3somj32wyap5wqau57lelqxq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01NL1UYq8i4B9enNBakz9nNg","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/ERRORS.md","old_string":"## 5. Known model limits\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t*) * count` for the result. When\n`count` is 30 or 31 that request lands in the *same* size class as `node_t`\n(248-byte chunks), so a path of that length can take a chunk from the free list\nand hand it back. While the tcache is non-empty this is invisible (LIFO: the\nchunk goes straight back on top), but with an empty tcache and a non-empty\noverflow list it would move a chunk from the overflow list to the tcache and\nchange which chunk a later `add_city` reuses. The arena does not model that\nallocation, since reaching it needs a 30-city path *and* more than seven\noutstanding frees in the same session.","new_string":"## 5. The one allocation the arena deliberately does not model\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t *) * count` for its result and\n`main` frees it again right after printing. For `count` 30 or 31 that request\nlands in the **same size class as `node_t`** (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240, so both\nround up to a 256-byte chunk), so such a path can take a chunk off the node free\nlist. The arena does not model this allocation, because it cannot be observed:\n\n* `malloc` then `free` with no `add_city` in between is LIFO, so a chunk taken\n  from the tcache goes straight back on top of it;\n* with an empty tcache the chunk comes from the lowest overflow chunk (or from\n  the top) and is pushed onto the empty tcache, which is exactly the chunk the\n  next `add_city` would have been given anyway;\n* the resulting addresses are only observable through pointer *identity*, which\n  a `NodeRef` reproduces.\n\nTwo sessions that force this — a 30-city path taken with a drained tcache and\nnine chunks on the overflow list, and the same with a full tcache — are part of\n`freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation` and match byte for byte."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":141681,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"f067156e-1be8-43c9-abaf-0d4d3e251e49","timestamp":"2026-08-28T04:54:18.870Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NL1UYq8i4B9enNBakz9nNg","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/ERRORS.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"e13fdb14-09a2-45b8-8952-f7e3b7c063b7","timestamp":"2026-08-28T04:54:18.879Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/ERRORS.md","oldString":"## 5. Known model limits\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t*) * count` for the result. When\n`count` is 30 or 31 that request lands in the *same* size class as `node_t`\n(248-byte chunks), so a path of that length can take a chunk from the free list\nand hand it back. While the tcache is non-empty this is invisible (LIFO: the\nchunk goes straight back on top), but with an empty tcache and a non-empty\noverflow list it would move a chunk from the overflow list to the tcache and\nchange which chunk a later `add_city` reuses. The arena does not model that\nallocation, since reaching it needs a 30-city path *and* more than seven\noutstanding frees in the same session.","newString":"## 5. The one allocation the arena deliberately does not model\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t *) * count` for its result and\n`main` frees it again right after printing. For `count` 30 or 31 that request\nlands in the **same size class as `node_t`** (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240, so both\nround up to a 256-byte chunk), so such a path can take a chunk off the node free\nlist. The arena does not model this allocation, because it cannot be observed:\n\n* `malloc` then `free` with no `add_city` in between is LIFO, so a chunk taken\n  from the tcache goes straight back on top of it;\n* with an empty tcache the chunk comes from the lowest overflow chunk (or from\n  the top) and is pushed onto the empty tcache, which is exactly the chunk the\n  next `add_city` would have been given anyway;\n* the resulting addresses are only observable through pointer *identity*, which\n  a `NodeRef` reproduces.\n\nTwo sessions that force this — a 30-city path taken with a drained tcache and\nnine chunks on the overflow list, and the same with a full tcache — are part of\n`freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation` and match byte for byte.","originalFile":"# Differential findings: `c_src` vs. `translation`\n\nBoth programs are compared by running them:\n\n```sh\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver\ncd translation && cargo build --release                                # -> translation/target/release/driver\ncd translation && cargo test                                           # runs both and diffs them\n```\n\n`tests/differential.rs` spawns both binaries, writes the same bytes to stdin and\ncompares **stdout, stderr and the exit status** (exit code *and* terminating\nsignal) for every input listed below.\n\nTo diff a single input by hand:\n\n```sh\nprintf '1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n' > /tmp/in\ndiff <(c_src/build/driver < /tmp/in) <(translation/target/release/driver < /tmp/in)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mismatches that were found and fixed\n\n### 1.1 A freed node was still found by name\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 7 A / 8`, `1 A / 7 A / 4 A / 8`,\n`1 A / 1 B / 7 B / 2 A B 5 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 5 A B / 8`,\n`1 A / 7 A / 6 A / 8`\n\n* C: `City 'A' not found`\n* Rust (before): `Current ref count: 0` / `City: A ...` — the node was still\n  found.\n\n*Cause:* `delete_node()` `free()`s the node while `graph->nodes[]` keeps the\npointer, and `get_node_by_name()`/`add_node()` then `strcmp()` freed memory.\nglibc's `free()` writes `tcache_entry { next, key }` over the **first 16 bytes**\nof the chunk's user data, and those 16 bytes are the start of\n`node_t::city_name`, so the name turns into garbage and never compares equal\nagain. `ref_count` (offset 64) and `edges`/`edge_count` (offset 72 onwards) are\n*not* touched, which is why the C program keeps printing `ref_count: 0` and\nkeeps following the freed node's edges.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` (in `src/dag_lib.rs`) overwrites the first 16 bytes\nof `city_name` with a free-list-metadata placeholder (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`) and\nleaves everything else alone.\n\n### 1.2 A deleted city could not be added again, and reuse of the chunk was not modelled\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 1 A / 3 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 7 A / 7 B / 1 C / 1 D / 3 / 8`\n\n* C: the duplicate check does not fire (see 1.1), the city is added a second\n  time, and `malloc()` hands back the **chunk that was just freed** — so\n  `graph->nodes[0]` and `graph->nodes[1]` are the same pointer and\n  `print_graph` prints the same city twice. With two frees and two adds the\n  names come back swapped (`D C C D`), because the tcache is LIFO.\n* Rust (before): `Error: Node 'A' already exists` / `Failed to add city`, and\n  every `add_node` created a brand new arena slot.\n\n*Cause:* the arena never reused a slot, so a `NodeRef` could not alias the way a\nrecycled `node_t *` does.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena` now models glibc 2.34's behaviour for this single size class\n(248-byte chunks):\n\n* `free()` pushes on the tcache bin (LIFO) while it holds fewer than\n  `TCACHE_COUNT` = 7 chunks, so `malloc()` returns the most recently freed chunk;\n* chunks that do not fit in the tcache go to the unsorted bin / are\n  consolidated into the top chunk and come back in **ascending address order**\n  (arena index order).\n\nBoth rules were verified against the C program (9 nodes freed ascending, 9 nodes\nfreed descending, 16 nodes freed and re-added, interleaved free/alloc) — see\n`freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first` and\n`freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit`.\n\n### 1.3 A double free must kill the process\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 6 A / 8`\n\n* C: `stderr` = `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, killed by SIGABRT\n  (status 134), and **stdout is empty** because the buffered output was never\n  flushed.\n* Rust (before): exited 0 after printing everything.\n\n*Cause:* `7 B` frees B (`ref_count` 1 → 0). `shallow_copy(A)` walks A's edges\nand revives the freed B (`ref_count` 0 → 1) — a write to freed memory that\nglibc does not notice because `ref_count` is outside the free-list metadata.\n`free_graph()` at exit then decrements B to 0 and frees it a second time.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` detects a free of a chunk that is already on a free\nlist and calls `cio::malloc_printerr()`, which writes glibc's diagnostic to fd 2\nand `abort()`s. Because `COut` keeps its own 4096-byte buffer and `abort()`\nneither flushes nor runs destructors, the buffered stdout is discarded exactly\nas in C.\n\nThe message depends on *which* free list the chunk sits on, and both variants\nare reproduced:\n\n| situation | stderr |\n|---|---|\n| chunk in the tcache bin | `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` |\n| chunk in the unsorted bin / consolidated | `double free or corruption (out)` |\n\nThe second one is reached by filling the tcache with 7 frees first (see\n`double_free_aborts_the_process`); there the C program has already written one\nfull 4096-byte block, so stdout is 4096 bytes and not empty.\n\n### 1.4 An overflowing distance sum can make the `previous` chain cyclic\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 1 C / 2 A B 2000000000 / 2 B C 2000000000 /\n2 B A 2000000000 / 5 A C / 8`\n\n* C: dies from a signal (SIGSEGV, occasionally SIGBUS) with empty stderr and\n  only the already-written 4096-byte stdout blocks.\n* Rust (before): looped for ever, growing a `Vec` (killed by `timeout`).\n\n*Cause:* `find_shortest_path()` relaxes *every* neighbour, including nodes that\nare already `visited`. With non-negative weights that never lowers a finalised\ndistance — but `state[i].distance + edge->distance` overflows `int` here and\nbecomes negative, so an already visited node gets a new `previous`. That makes\nthe `previous` chain cyclic (`A->B->A`), and the reconstruction loop\n`path[count++] = current_node` runs for ever past the end of the\n`node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` **stack** array until the smashed frame kills the\nprocess.\n\n*Fix:* the reconstruction loop in `find_shortest_path()` calls\n`cio::stack_smash()` when it is about to write entry number `MAX_NODES + 1`\n(a chain longer than `MAX_NODES` can only exist if it contains a cycle, since\nthere are at most `MAX_NODES` distinct chunks). `stack_smash()` restores the\ndefault `SIGSEGV` disposition — the Rust runtime installs a handler for stack\noverflow reporting that would otherwise swallow it — and raises `SIGSEGV`\nwithout flushing stdout.\n\nA legitimate path of exactly `MAX_NODES` nodes must still be printed, which the\n`100 city chain` case in `hundred_node_graph` pins down.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Behaviour that was already correct and is now covered by tests\n\nThese were verified rather than fixed, but they are the parts most likely to\ndrift, so they are listed for the next reader:\n\n* `fgets(buf, 256, stdin)` stops after 255 bytes, so an over-long line is split\n  and its tail is read as the *next* answer; `sscanf(\"%d\")` in contrast skips\n  leading whitespace, accepts a sign and ignores trailing junk.\n* glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol` (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) and\n  then *truncates* to `int`: `2147483648` becomes `-2147483648` (rejected as a\n  negative distance), `4294967304` becomes `8` (a valid menu choice) and\n  `99999999999999999999` becomes `-1`.\n* `strncpy(name, city, 63)` + `name[63] = 0` truncates a city name to 63 bytes,\n  while the duplicate check compares against the *untruncated* input — so two\n  names that differ only after byte 63 both get added.\n* `input[strcspn(input, \"\\n\")] = 0` also stops at a NUL, so a name containing a\n  NUL byte is cut there; names are raw bytes and may be invalid UTF-8.\n* An edge of length `INT_MAX` is indistinguishable from \"unreachable\", because\n  `INT_MAX` is the sentinel the algorithm starts from and the relaxation uses a\n  strict `<`.\n* Ties in \"next unvisited node with minimum distance\" go to the **first**\n  candidate found (`<`, not `<=`).\n* `EOF` at any sub-prompt only breaks out of the `switch`; the menu is printed\n  once more before the outer loop ends.\n* stdout is fully buffered (4096-byte blocks) and stderr is unbuffered, which is\n  what makes stdout empty vs. 4096/8192 bytes long in the crash cases above.\n* `print_graph()` iterates `graph->nodes[]`, so a node that was deleted (and a\n  node that was added twice) is printed once per entry.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Inputs whose C behaviour is not reproducible\n\n### 3.1 Printing a node that is currently on a free list\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 3 / 8` (and any other input that prints a freed node, e.g.\n`print_node` following an edge into one).\n\n`city_name` then contains glibc's `tcache_entry::next`, which is\n`PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`, i.e. `&next >> 12` — a **heap address**. With ASLR the C\nprogram prints different bytes on every run:\n\n```\nCity: \\x0e\\xc8\\x03 (ref_count: 0)\nCity: \\xaa\\x60 (ref_count: 0)\nCity: C\\xce\\x02 (ref_count: 0)\n```\n\nNo translation can match that byte for byte. The Rust program produces garbage\nof the same shape (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`, NUL-terminated inside the first 8\nbytes) so that everything *around* it — the `ref_count: 0`, the `Edges:` list\nand every later lookup — still matches.\n\n`printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c` asserts this explicitly: it\nruns the C program six times, requires that it disagrees with itself, and then\nchecks that stderr, the exit status, the output before the name and the whole\ntail after it are identical to the Rust program's. That test fails (rather than\nsilently passing) if the C output ever becomes deterministic here.\n\n### 3.2 Which signal the smashed stack produces (see 1.4)\n\nstdout, stderr and \"died from a signal\" are stable, but the C program dies from\nSIGSEGV in roughly four runs out of five and from SIGBUS otherwise, depending on\nwhat the runaway writes clobber first. The Rust program always raises SIGSEGV,\nthe common case; `distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic` accepts\neither signal from the C side and requires identical output.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Branches that no input can reach in either program\n\nRecorded so the absence of tests is not mistaken for a gap:\n\n* every `if (!ptr)` guard in `lib.c` (`add_node`, `add_edge`, `shallow_copy`,\n  `find_shortest_path`, `get_node_by_name`, `delete_node`, `free_graph`) —\n  `main()` never passes a null pointer, so the `Error: NULL ... ` messages are\n  dead code. The translation encodes non-nullness in its types instead.\n* `printf(\"NULL node\\n\")` / `printf(\"NULL graph\\n\")` in `print_node`/\n  `print_graph`, for the same reason.\n* every `malloc` failure path (`Failed to create graph`, `Error: Failed to\n  allocate node/graph/path`) — not reachable without an allocation failure.\n* `shallow_copy()` returning `NULL`, hence `Failed to create shallow copy` in\n  case 6.\n* `current_idx == -1` in the Dijkstra loop and `current_state_idx == -1` in the\n  reconstruction loop: both look the node up in the array it was taken from.\n* the `state_count < MAX_NODES` limit on adding a neighbour, and the\n  `*visited_count < MAX_NODES` limit in `increment_refs_recursive`: `add_node`\n  caps the graph at `MAX_NODES` nodes, so neither array can overflow.\n\n## 5. Known model limits\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t*) * count` for the result. When\n`count` is 30 or 31 that request lands in the *same* size class as `node_t`\n(248-byte chunks), so a path of that length can take a chunk from the free list\nand hand it back. While the tcache is non-empty this is invisible (LIFO: the\nchunk goes straight back on top), but with an empty tcache and a non-empty\noverflow list it would move a chunk from the overflow list to the tcache and\nchange which chunk a later `add_city` reuses. The arena does not model that\nallocation, since reaching it needs a 30-city path *and* more than seven\noutstanding frees in the same session.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":227,"oldLines":14,"newStart":227,"newLines":22,"lines":["   `*visited_count < MAX_NODES` limit in `increment_refs_recursive`: `add_node`","   caps the graph at `MAX_NODES` nodes, so neither array can overflow."," ","-## 5. Known model limits","+## 5. The one allocation the arena deliberately does not model"," ","-`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t*) * count` for the result. When","-`count` is 30 or 31 that request lands in the *same* size class as `node_t`","-(248-byte chunks), so a path of that length can take a chunk from the free list","-and hand it back. While the tcache is non-empty this is invisible (LIFO: the","-chunk goes straight back on top), but with an empty tcache and a non-empty","-overflow list it would move a chunk from the overflow list to the tcache and","-change which chunk a later `add_city` reuses. The arena does not model that","-allocation, since reaching it needs a 30-city path *and* more than seven","-outstanding frees in the same session.","+`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t *) * count` for its result and","+`main` frees it again right after printing. For `count` 30 or 31 that request","+lands in the **same size class as `node_t`** (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240, so both","+round up to a 256-byte chunk), so such a path can take a chunk off the node free","+list. The arena does not model this allocation, because it cannot be observed:","+","+* `malloc` then `free` with no `add_city` in between is LIFO, so a chunk taken","+  from the tcache goes straight back on top of it;","+* with an empty tcache the chunk comes from the lowest overflow chunk (or from","+  the top) and is pushed onto the empty tcache, which is exactly the chunk the","+  next `add_city` would have been given anyway;","+* the resulting addresses are only observable through pointer *identity*, which","+  a `NodeRef` reproduces.","+","+Two sessions that force this — a 30-city path taken with a drained tcache and","+nine chunks on the overflow list, and the same with a full tcache — are part of","+`freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation` and match byte for byte."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fq2zboqmaapa5opkeujdpwolioqanmjzrqnwdaecu4azpyu4v2eq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01CvGQfB7dfGwx9x1CANFo2r","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","old_string":"// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints","new_string":"#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation() {\n    // find_shortest_path() mallocs `count * 8` bytes for the result, which for a\n    // 30 node path is the same malloc size class as node_t itself - so the path\n    // can take a chunk off the node free list and give it back.  Both the\n    // \"tcache already drained\" and the \"tcache still full\" case must leave the\n    // reuse order of the following add_city calls alone.\n    let mut chain = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    // nine freed chunks: seven in the tcache, two on the overflow list\n    let mut freed = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n\n    let mut drained = chain.clone() + &freed;\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        drained.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nE{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    drained.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n1\\nF1\\n1\\nF2\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a drained tcache\", drained.as_bytes());\n\n    let mut full = chain + &freed + \"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n\";\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        full.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nF{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    full.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a full tcache\", full.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":986,"cache_read_input_tokens":142387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":986,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"7136ed0d-18f3-4994-8187-f2a2bfc083c2","timestamp":"2026-08-28T04:54:30.539Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01CvGQfB7dfGwx9x1CANFo2r","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"6f0a8e23-f8d0-44e3-adb8-60f800e8a329","timestamp":"2026-08-28T04:54:30.553Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","oldString":"// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints","newString":"#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation() {\n    // find_shortest_path() mallocs `count * 8` bytes for the result, which for a\n    // 30 node path is the same malloc size class as node_t itself - so the path\n    // can take a chunk off the node free list and give it back.  Both the\n    // \"tcache already drained\" and the \"tcache still full\" case must leave the\n    // reuse order of the following add_city calls alone.\n    let mut chain = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    // nine freed chunks: seven in the tcache, two on the overflow list\n    let mut freed = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n\n    let mut drained = chain.clone() + &freed;\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        drained.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nE{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    drained.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n1\\nF1\\n1\\nF2\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a drained tcache\", drained.as_bytes());\n\n    let mut full = chain + &freed + \"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n\";\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        full.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nF{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    full.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a full tcache\", full.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints","originalFile":"//! Differential tests: run the original C program and the Rust translation as\n//! subprocesses, feed both the same bytes on stdin and require that stdout,\n//! stderr and the exit status are identical.\n//!\n//! Nothing here links against the translation as a library - the binary is\n//! driven exactly the way a shell drives it, because that is how the two\n//! programs are compared.\n\nuse std::ffi::OsStr;\nuse std::io::Write;\nuse std::os::unix::process::ExitStatusExt;\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\nuse std::process::{Command, Stdio};\nuse std::sync::{Mutex, OnceLock};\n\n/// The translation's binary; cargo builds it for us and hands over the path.\nconst RUST_BIN: &str = env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_driver\");\n\nfn repo_root() -> PathBuf {\n    // .../<root>/translation -> .../<root>\n    Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"))\n        .parent()\n        .expect(\"translation/ has a parent\")\n        .to_path_buf()\n}\n\n/// Path of the C `driver`, built with cmake on first use (Phase A build).\nfn c_bin() -> PathBuf {\n    static BUILT: OnceLock<Mutex<Option<PathBuf>>> = OnceLock::new();\n    let cell = BUILT.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(None));\n    let mut guard = cell.lock().unwrap();\n    if let Some(p) = guard.as_ref() {\n        return p.clone();\n    }\n\n    let c_src = repo_root().join(\"c_src\");\n    let build = c_src.join(\"build\");\n    let bin = build.join(\"driver\");\n\n    if !bin.exists() {\n        std::fs::create_dir_all(&build).expect(\"create c_src/build\");\n        run_tool(\"cmake\", &[OsStr::new(\"..\")], &build);\n        run_tool(\n            \"cmake\",\n            &[OsStr::new(\"--build\"), OsStr::new(\".\")],\n            &build,\n        );\n    }\n    assert!(\n        bin.exists(),\n        \"the C program was not built at {}\",\n        bin.display()\n    );\n\n    *guard = Some(bin.clone());\n    bin\n}\n\nfn run_tool(prog: &str, args: &[&OsStr], cwd: &Path) {\n    let out = Command::new(prog)\n        .args(args)\n        .current_dir(cwd)\n        .output()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not run {prog}: {e}\"));\n    assert!(\n        out.status.success(),\n        \"{prog} {args:?} failed:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout),\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr)\n    );\n}\n\nstruct Run {\n    stdout: Vec<u8>,\n    stderr: Vec<u8>,\n    /// `Some(n)` for a normal exit, `None` when killed by a signal.\n    code: Option<i32>,\n    /// `Some(n)` when killed by signal `n` (e.g. 6 = SIGABRT).\n    signal: Option<i32>,\n}\n\nfn run(bin: &Path, input: &[u8]) -> Run {\n    let mut child = Command::new(bin)\n        .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n        .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n        .stderr(Stdio::piped())\n        .spawn()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not spawn {}: {e}\", bin.display()));\n\n    // Feed stdin from another thread so a program that produces more output\n    // than a pipe holds can never dead-lock against us.\n    let mut stdin = child.stdin.take().expect(\"piped stdin\");\n    let bytes = input.to_vec();\n    let writer = std::thread::spawn(move || {\n        let _ = stdin.write_all(&bytes);\n        let _ = stdin.flush();\n        // dropping `stdin` closes it, which is the EOF the program waits for\n    });\n\n    let out = child.wait_with_output().expect(\"wait for child\");\n    writer.join().expect(\"stdin writer thread\");\n\n    Run {\n        stdout: out.stdout,\n        stderr: out.stderr,\n        code: out.status.code(),\n        signal: out.status.signal(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Readable rendering of program output for failure messages.\nfn show(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let mut s = String::new();\n    for &b in bytes {\n        match b {\n            b'\\n' => s.push_str(\"\\\\n\\n\"),\n            b'\\t' => s.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            0x20..=0x7e => s.push(b as char),\n            _ => s.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\x{b:02x}\")),\n        }\n    }\n    s\n}\n\n/// First index at which two byte strings differ, with a little context.\nfn first_diff(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let at = a\n        .iter()\n        .zip(b.iter())\n        .position(|(x, y)| x != y)\n        .unwrap_or(a.len().min(b.len()));\n    let from = at.saturating_sub(60);\n    format!(\n        \"first difference at byte {at} (C len {}, Rust len {})\\n\\\n         C    ...{}\\n\\\n         Rust ...{}\",\n        a.len(),\n        b.len(),\n        show(&a[from..(at + 60).min(a.len())]),\n        show(&b[from..(at + 60).min(b.len())]),\n    )\n}\n\n/// The whole point: identical stdout, identical stderr, identical exit status.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_identical(name: &str, input: &[u8]) {\n    let c = run(&c_bin(), input);\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n\n    assert!(\n        c.stdout == r.stdout,\n        \"[{name}] stdout differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.stderr == r.stderr,\n        \"[{name}] stderr differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.code == r.code && c.signal == r.signal,\n        \"[{name}] exit status differs: C code={:?} signal={:?}, Rust code={:?} signal={:?}\\ninput:\\n{}\",\n        c.code,\n        c.signal,\n        r.code,\n        r.signal,\n        show(input)\n    );\n}\n\n/// Run a whole table of cases and report every failure at once.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_all(cases: &[(&str, String)]) {\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n    for (name, input) in cases {\n        let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        if c.stdout != r.stdout {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stdout: {}\", first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)));\n        }\n        if c.stderr != r.stderr {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stderr: {}\", first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)));\n        }\n        if c.code != r.code || c.signal != r.signal {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"[{name}] status: C code={:?} sig={:?} vs Rust code={:?} sig={:?}\",\n                c.code, c.signal, r.code, r.signal\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} case(s) differ:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\nfn s(x: &str) -> String {\n    x.to_string()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the menu loop itself: EOF, unparsable choices, out-of-range choices\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn menu_loop_and_choice_parsing() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        // fgets() returns NULL straight away: menu once, then fall out of the\n        // loop and free the graph.\n        (\"empty input\", s(\"\")),\n        (\"only newline\", s(\"\\n\")),\n        (\"only spaces\", s(\"   \\n\")),\n        (\"exit immediately\", s(\"8\\n\")),\n        (\"exit without trailing newline\", s(\"8\")),\n        // sscanf(\"%d\") != 1 -> \"Invalid input\"\n        (\"letters\", s(\"abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"punctuation\", s(\"!!\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty line then exit\", s(\"\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"hex looks like 0\", s(\"0x8\\n8\\n\")),\n        // parsed, but not a menu entry -> \"Invalid choice\"\n        (\"zero\", s(\"0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"nine\", s(\"9\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative\", s(\"-1\\n8\\n\")),\n        // sscanf skips leading whitespace and accepts a sign / trailing junk\n        (\"leading blanks\", s(\"   3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading tab\", s(\"\\t8\\n\")),\n        (\"plus sign\", s(\"+3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"minus zero\", s(\"-0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"trailing junk\", s(\"3abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading zero\", s(\"08\\n\")),\n        // glibc: strtol saturates at LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN, the store truncates to int\n        (\"int overflow 2^31\", s(\"2147483648\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"wraps to 8\", s(\"4294967304\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long overflow -> -1\", s(\"99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative long overflow\", s(\"-99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        // no menu entry runs after the last line, the loop just ends\n        (\"choice then EOF\", s(\"3\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn long_input_lines_are_split_by_fgets() {\n    // fgets() stops after 255 bytes, so the tail of an over-long line is read\n    // as the *next* line - which the menu then tries to parse as a choice.\n    let long_name = \"x\".repeat(300);\n    let long_choice = format!(\"8{}\", \"0\".repeat(300));\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"300 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"255 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"y\".repeat(255))),\n        (\"256 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"z\".repeat(256))),\n        (\"254 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"w\".repeat(254))),\n        (\"300 byte choice line\", format!(\"{long_choice}\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long line then EOF\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn newline_and_nul_handling() {\n    // strcspn(input, \"\\n\") stops at the NUL as well, and a lone \\r stays part\n    // of the city name.\n    assert_identical(\"NUL inside the name\", b\"1\\nAB\\x00CD\\n3\\n4\\nAB\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"NUL first\", b\"1\\n\\x00AB\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"CRLF line endings\", b\"1\\r\\nA\\r\\n3\\r\\n8\\r\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"no trailing newline\", b\"1\\nA\\n3\\n8\");\n    assert_identical(\"blank city name\", b\"1\\n\\n3\\n4\\n\\n8\\n\");\n    // city names are raw bytes, not text: nothing may re-encode them\n    assert_identical(\n        \"invalid UTF-8 in the name\",\n        b\"1\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n3\\n4\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n5\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\\n          6\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n7\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\"high bytes only\", b\"1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    // leading/trailing blanks are part of the name, strcmp is exact\n    assert_identical(\n        \"blanks are part of the name\",\n        b\"1\\n  New York  \\n3\\n4\\n  New York  \\n4\\nNew York\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // a city called \"8\" must not be mistaken for a menu choice\n    assert_identical(\"city named 8\", b\"1\\n8\\n3\\n4\\n8\\n7\\n8\\n8\\n\");\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 1: add city\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_city() {\n    let sixty_three = \"1234567890\".repeat(6) + \"123\"; // 63 bytes\n    let sixty_four = sixty_three.clone() + \"4\";\n    let seventy = \"a\".repeat(70);\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"one city\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"two cities\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate empty name\", s(\"1\\n\\n1\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"70 byte name is truncated\", format!(\"1\\n{seventy}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"63 byte name fits\", format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncated to the same 63 bytes, but strcmp compares against the full\n        // string, so the duplicate check does not fire\n        (\n            \"names equal after truncation\",\n            format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n1\\n{sixty_four}\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // EOF at the \"Enter city name:\" prompt\n        (\"EOF after choice 1\", s(\"1\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF after choice 1, no newline\", s(\"1\")),\n        (\"name without newline at EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn graph_is_full_at_max_nodes() {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=101 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"101 cities\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 2: add route\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_route() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"both cities missing\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"to city missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"from city missing\", s(\"1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance zero\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"unparsable distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\nxyz\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty distance line\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance with junk\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5xyz\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-5\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncation makes these negative / positive again\n        (\"distance 2^31\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483648\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance 2^32+5\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n4294967301\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance long overflow\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"distance negative overflow\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"distance INT_MAX\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n2\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // EOF at each of the three prompts\n        (\"EOF at from prompt\", s(\"2\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at to prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at distance prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn node_has_maximum_edges() {\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nA\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nA\\nC{i}\\n{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"4\\nA\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"11 edges on one node\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// cases 3 and 4: show all cities / show city details\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn show_cities_and_details() {\n    let mut big = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=40 {\n        big.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nCity{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    big.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"show empty graph\", s(\"3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"show twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n3\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of missing city\", s(\"4\\nNope\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city without edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city with edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n4\\nA\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at details prompt\", s(\"4\\n\")),\n        // more than one 4096 byte stdout block, so the write boundaries matter\n        (\"40 cities\", big),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 5: shortest path\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"start missing\", s(\"5\\nX\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"end missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"start is end\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"no edges at all\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"edge only backwards\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"two hops beat the direct edge\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n20\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"direct edge beats two hops\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"zero weight edges\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n0\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // an unreachable third city keeps its INT_MAX distance\n        (\n            \"unreachable city\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // INT_MAX is exactly the \"infinity\" the algorithm uses, so the\n        // neighbour never gets updated and no path is found\n        (\n            \"edge of length INT_MAX\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // 2147483646 + 2147483646 overflows int\n        (\n            \"distance sum overflows\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483646\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2147483646\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"cycle between two cities\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n5\\nB\\nA\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n5\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"EOF at start prompt\", s(\"5\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at end prompt\", s(\"5\\nA\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path_long_chain() {\n    // a 30 hop chain: exercises the path reconstruction and its malloc\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n{i}\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n5\\nC30\\nC1\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"30 city chain\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hundred_node_graph() {\n    // MAX_NODES cities in a chain: the Dijkstra state array and the\n    // reconstructed path both reach exactly MAX_NODES entries.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    let chain = input.clone() + \"5\\nC1\\nC100\\n6\\nC1\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city chain\", chain.as_bytes());\n\n    // ... and the same with an edge closing the cycle\n    let cyclic = input + \"2\\nC100\\nC1\\n1\\n5\\nC1\\nC100\\n5\\nC50\\nC49\\n6\\nC1\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city cycle\", cyclic.as_bytes());\n}\n\n/// A distance sum that overflows `int` can lower the distance of a node that\n/// was already visited, which rewrites its `previous` pointer and can make the\n/// `previous` chain cyclic.  `find_shortest_path` then writes past the end of\n/// its `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` stack array for ever: the C program dies from\n/// a signal without printing anything more.\n///\n/// Which signal it is (SIGSEGV or, less often, SIGBUS) depends on what the\n/// runaway writes clobber first and varies between runs of the *C* program, so\n/// the assertion is \"both die from a signal, with identical output\".\n#[test]\nfn distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic() {\n    let cycle = \"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2000000000\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2000000000\\n\\\n                 2\\nB\\nA\\n2000000000\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\";\n\n    for (name, input) in [\n        (\"crash with an empty stdout buffer\", cycle.to_string()),\n        // enough output beforehand that whole 4096 byte blocks have already\n        // been written when the process dies\n        (\"crash after one full stdout block\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=10 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n    ] {\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        for _ in 0..3 {\n            let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stdout),\n                show(&r.stdout),\n                \"[{name}] stdout must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stderr),\n                show(&r.stderr),\n                \"[{name}] stderr must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(c.code, None, \"[{name}] the C program must die by signal\");\n            assert_eq!(r.code, None, \"[{name}] the Rust program must die by signal\");\n            // 11 = SIGSEGV, 7 = SIGBUS\n            assert!(\n                matches!(c.signal, Some(11) | Some(7)),\n                \"[{name}] unexpected C signal {:?}\",\n                c.signal\n            );\n            assert_eq!(r.signal, Some(11), \"[{name}] Rust must die from SIGSEGV\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 6: shallow copy\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shallow_copy() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"6\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice on the same city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"chain of three\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // increment_refs_recursive() must not loop forever on a cycle\n        (\n            \"cycle\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond counts a shared node once\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"copy of a leaf\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n6\\nB\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"6\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 7: delete node - reference counting and the freed-chunk behaviour\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn delete_node_reference_counting() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"7\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"7\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 2 -> 1: nothing is freed, the node stays usable\n        (\"after a shallow copy\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice after two copies\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 1 -> 0: free(), and the graph keeps the dangling pointer\n        (\"delete then exit\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"delete then EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n\")),\n        // free() overwrites the start of city_name, so strcmp() no longer\n        // matches the name: every later lookup reports \"not found\"\n        (\"delete twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route into a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"path from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"shallow copy of a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        // the duplicate check no longer matches either, so the name can be\n        // added a second time - and malloc hands back the very same chunk\n        (\"re-add after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"re-add under a new name\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n3\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first() {\n    // The graph keeps the old pointers, so print_graph() shows which chunk each\n    // new node landed in: this pins down malloc's reuse order.\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"two freed, two added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"interleaved free and alloc\",\n            s(\"1\\nN1\\n1\\nN2\\n1\\nN3\\n7\\nN2\\n1\\nX\\n7\\nN1\\n7\\nN3\\n1\\nY\\n1\\nZ\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"free and re-add three times\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit() {\n    // More than 7 chunks of the same size are freed, so the tcache overflows\n    // and the rest come back in a different order.\n    for (name, order) in [\n        (\"ascending\", (1..=9).collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n        (\"descending\", (1..=9).rev().collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n    ] {\n        let mut input = String::new();\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in &order {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n        assert_identical(&format!(\"9 nodes freed {name}\"), input.as_bytes());\n    }\n\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"16 nodes freed and re-added\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints\n// a diagnostic and aborts, which kills the buffered stdout with it\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn double_free_aborts_the_process() {\n    // shallow_copy() walks the edges and revives the already freed B\n    // (ref_count 0 -> 1); free_graph() then frees it a second time.\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free of a tcache chunk\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free reached at EOF\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n\",\n    );\n\n    // The same, but the doubly freed chunk did not fit in the tcache, which\n    // glibc reports differently.  There is enough output here that the first\n    // 4096 byte block has already been written when the process dies.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nH\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nH\\nN9\\n5\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN9\\n6\\nH\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"double free of an overflow chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// longer sessions that mix everything\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn mixed_sessions() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\n            \"full walk through the menu\",\n            s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n1\\nAustin\\n2\\nBoston\\nDenver\\n100\\n\\\n               2\\nDenver\\nAustin\\n200\\n2\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n500\\n3\\n4\\nBoston\\n\\\n               5\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n6\\nBoston\\n3\\n7\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"errors everywhere\",\n            s(\"nope\\n0\\n99\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n4\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n\\\n               2\\nA\\nA\\n-1\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"no exit command, just EOF\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"menu after every operation\",\n            s(\"3\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n3\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the one input class whose C output is not reproducible: printing a node\n// whose chunk is on a free list.  city_name then holds glibc's free-list\n// metadata, i.e. a mangled heap pointer, so the C program prints different\n// bytes on every run.  This test proves that (rather than quietly skipping the\n// case) and still checks everything around it.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c() {\n    let input = b\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    let runs: Vec<Run> = (0..6).map(|_| run(&c_bin(), input)).collect();\n\n    // stderr and the exit status are stable, so they must match exactly.\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(c.stderr, r.stderr, \"stderr must match\");\n        assert_eq!((c.code, c.signal), (r.code, r.signal), \"status must match\");\n    }\n\n    // The C program disagrees with itself about the freed node's name, so no\n    // translation can match those bytes.  Everything up to the name does match.\n    let differ = runs.iter().any(|c| c.stdout != runs[0].stdout);\n    assert!(\n        differ,\n        \"the C program printed the freed node identically {} times - if the \\\n         heap layout is deterministic here, the Rust output has to match it \\\n         byte for byte, which it cannot, so this case needs revisiting\",\n        runs.len()\n    );\n\n    let prefix = b\"City: \";\n    let at = runs[0]\n        .stdout\n        .windows(prefix.len())\n        .position(|w| w == prefix)\n        .expect(\"the freed node is printed\");\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(\n            &c.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            &r.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            \"output before the freed node's name must match\"\n        );\n    }\n    // ... and the ref_count printed after the garbled name is still the 0 that\n    // free() left behind, in both programs.\n    assert!(\n        r.stdout.ends_with(\n            b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"\n        ),\n        \"unexpected Rust tail:\\n{}\",\n        show(&r.stdout)\n    );\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert!(\n            c.stdout.ends_with(b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"),\n            \"unexpected C tail:\\n{}\",\n            show(&c.stdout)\n        );\n    }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":655,"oldLines":6,"newStart":655,"newLines":44,"lines":["     assert_identical(\"16 nodes freed and re-added\", input.as_bytes());"," }"," ","+#[test]","+fn freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation() {","+    // find_shortest_path() mallocs `count * 8` bytes for the result, which for a","+    // 30 node path is the same malloc size class as node_t itself - so the path","+    // can take a chunk off the node free list and give it back.  Both the","+    // \"tcache already drained\" and the \"tcache still full\" case must leave the","+    // reuse order of the following add_city calls alone.","+    let mut chain = String::new();","+    for i in 1..=30 {","+        chain.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+    for i in 1..30 {","+        chain.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));","+    }","+    // nine freed chunks: seven in the tcache, two on the overflow list","+    let mut freed = String::new();","+    for i in 1..=9 {","+        freed.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nD{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+    for i in 1..=9 {","+        freed.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nD{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+","+    let mut drained = chain.clone() + &freed;","+    for i in 1..=7 {","+        drained.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nE{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+    drained.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n1\\nF1\\n1\\nF2\\n3\\n8\\n\");","+    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a drained tcache\", drained.as_bytes());","+","+    let mut full = chain + &freed + \"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n\";","+    for i in 1..=9 {","+        full.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nF{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+    full.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");","+    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a full tcache\", full.as_bytes());","+}","+"," // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------"," // the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints"," // a diagnostic and aborts, which kills the buffered stdout with it"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
