{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HGiGqRj9KNVCEZMbigKeNG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `c_src/src/lib.c` / `c_src/include/dag_lib.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C code hands out raw `node_t *` pointers, keeps them inside the graph\n4\t//! and (in `delete_node`) `free()`s a node while the graph still holds the\n5\t//! pointer.  Here every node lives in an arena (`Arena`) and a `node_t *` is\n6\t//! modelled as an index into that arena (`NodeRef`), so node identity\n7\t//! comparisons (`from->edges[i].destination == to`) keep working.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! Because the C program keeps *using* freed nodes, the arena also has to model\n10\t//! what glibc's `malloc`/`free` do to a freed `node_t` - see `Arena` for the\n11\t//! details.  Those effects are observable in the program's output and are\n12\t//! deterministic, so they are reproduced here.\n13\t\n14\tuse crate::cio::{eput, malloc_printerr, COut};\n15\t\n16\tpub const MAX_CITY_NAME: usize = 64;\n17\tpub const MAX_EDGES: usize = 10;\n18\tpub const MAX_NODES: usize = 100;\n19\t\n20\t/// Stand-in for `node_t *`.\n21\tpub type NodeRef = usize;\n22\t\n23\t/// `struct edge_t`\n24\tpub struct Edge {\n25\t    pub destination: NodeRef,\n26\t    pub distance: i32,\n27\t}\n28\t\n29\t/// `struct node_t`\n30\tpub struct Node {\n31\t    /// `char city_name[MAX_CITY_NAME]`\n32\t    pub city_name: [u8; MAX_CITY_NAME],\n33\t    pub ref_count: i32,\n34\t    pub edges: Vec<Edge>,\n35\t    /// Kept explicitly so the `edge_count >= MAX_EDGES` check mirrors the C.\n36\t    pub edge_count: i32,\n37\t    /// `true` while the chunk is on one of the allocator's free lists, i.e.\n38\t    /// between the `free()` in `delete_node` and the next `malloc()` that\n39\t    /// hands the very same chunk out again.\n40\t    pub freed: bool,\n41\t}\n42\t\n43\timpl Node {\n44\t    /// The value of the `city_name` C string (bytes up to the first NUL).\n45\t    pub fn name(&self) -> &[u8] {\n46\t        match self.city_name.iter().position(|&b| b == 0) {\n47\t            Some(i) => &self.city_name[..i],\n48\t            None => &self.city_name[..],\n49\t        }\n50\t    }\n51\t}\n52\t\n53\t/// glibc's `TCACHE_COUNT`: a size class caches at most 7 freed chunks.\n54\tconst TCACHE_COUNT: usize = 7;\n55\t\n56\t/// What glibc's `free()` leaves in the first 16 bytes of the user data of a\n57\t/// chunk it puts on a free list: `tcache_entry { next, key }` (or `fd`/`bk` for\n58\t/// the unsorted bin).  Those 16 bytes overlap `node_t::city_name`, which is why\n59\t/// a freed node's name turns into garbage and `strcmp()` in\n60\t/// `get_node_by_name()`/`add_node()` stops matching it.  `ref_count` (offset\n61\t/// 64) and `edges`/`edge_count` (offset 72 onwards) are *not* touched by\n62\t/// `free()`, so they keep their values - the C program relies on that.\n63\t///\n64\t/// The real bytes are heap addresses (`next` is `PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`, i.e.\n65\t/// `&next >> 12`), so they differ on every run because of ASLR; the placeholder\n66\t/// below only has to have the same shape: non-empty, NUL-terminated inside the\n67\t/// first 8 bytes and never equal to a city name a user could type.\n68\tconst FREE_LIST_METADATA: [u8; 16] = [\n69\t    0x0e, 0x8e, 0x38, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, // next = PROTECT_PTR(NULL)\n70\t    0xa0, 0x92, 0x55, 0x55, 0x55, 0x55, 0x00, 0x00, // key  = &tcache\n71\t];\n72\t\n73\t/// A maximal run of adjacent free chunks, `lo..=hi`, in address order - which\n74\t/// is arena index order, because chunks are carved out of the top chunk from\n75\t/// low to high.  glibc coalesces neighbouring free chunks, so its bins hold\n76\t/// runs like this rather than individual chunks.\n77\t#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n78\tstruct Run {\n79\t    lo: NodeRef,\n80\t    hi: NodeRef,\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\t/// Backing store for every `malloc`ed `node_t`: one entry per distinct heap\n84\t/// chunk, so a `NodeRef` behaves like the `node_t *` it stands for - including\n85\t/// the fact that a chunk which is freed and allocated again yields the *same*\n86\t/// pointer, which the C program's output exposes.\n87\t///\n88\t/// Every `node_t` lands in the same glibc size class (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240,\n89\t/// so the chunk is 256 bytes), and this is what glibc 2.34 does with such a\n90\t/// chunk - each rule below was checked against the C program:\n91\t///\n92\t///   * `free()` puts the chunk on the tcache bin as it is, while that bin holds\n93\t///     fewer than `TCACHE_COUNT` chunks, so `malloc()` hands the most recently\n94\t///     freed chunk back first;\n95\t///   * with a full tcache bin the chunk is coalesced with the adjacent free\n96\t///     runs (a chunk in the tcache still looks allocated and never takes part)\n97\t///     and then either absorbed into the top chunk, if it borders it, or put at\n98\t///     the head of the unsorted bin;\n99\t///   * `malloc()` with an empty tcache bin empties the unsorted bin oldest\n100\t///     first, moving same-sized chunks into the tcache and sorting bigger runs\n101\t///     into their size bin, and then returns the *last* chunk it cached - so\n102\t///     the newest of those chunks comes back first;\n103\t///   * a run that is too big is taken from its bin (smallest first) and split,\n104\t///     the caller gets its lowest chunk and the rest goes back to the unsorted\n105\t///     bin;\n106\t///   * `free()` writes free list metadata (`tcache_entry`, or `fd`/`bk`) over\n107\t///     the first 16 bytes of the chunk's user data, i.e. over the start of\n108\t///     `city_name` - but only for the chunk that actually goes on a list: a\n109\t///     chunk absorbed into the top chunk, or one that is merged into the middle\n110\t///     or the end of a run, keeps its name and stays findable by\n111\t///     `get_node_by_name()`;\n112\t///   * freeing a chunk that is already free is a double free: glibc prints a\n113\t///     diagnostic and aborts instead of returning.\n114\tpub struct Arena {\n115\t    pub nodes: Vec<Node>,\n116\t    /// How many chunks are carved out of the top chunk.  Freeing the highest\n117\t    /// chunk absorbs it back into the top chunk, which moves this down again;\n118\t    /// `nodes[top..]` is the storage inside the top chunk, which the C program\n119\t    /// can still read through a stale pointer.\n120\t    top: usize,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"80b69536-1275-4657-a96a-b7895f516b66","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:07:12.714Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/src/dag_lib.rs","content":"//! Translation of `c_src/src/lib.c` / `c_src/include/dag_lib.h`.\n//!\n//! The C code hands out raw `node_t *` pointers, keeps them inside the graph\n//! and (in `delete_node`) `free()`s a node while the graph still holds the\n//! pointer.  Here every node lives in an arena (`Arena`) and a `node_t *` is\n//! modelled as an index into that arena (`NodeRef`), so node identity\n//! comparisons (`from->edges[i].destination == to`) keep working.\n//!\n//! Because the C program keeps *using* freed nodes, the arena also has to model\n//! what glibc's `malloc`/`free` do to a freed `node_t` - see `Arena` for the\n//! details.  Those effects are observable in the program's output and are\n//! deterministic, so they are reproduced here.\n\nuse crate::cio::{eput, malloc_printerr, COut};\n\npub const MAX_CITY_NAME: usize = 64;\npub const MAX_EDGES: usize = 10;\npub const MAX_NODES: usize = 100;\n\n/// Stand-in for `node_t *`.\npub type NodeRef = usize;\n\n/// `struct edge_t`\npub struct Edge {\n    pub destination: NodeRef,\n    pub distance: i32,\n}\n\n/// `struct node_t`\npub struct Node {\n    /// `char city_name[MAX_CITY_NAME]`\n    pub city_name: [u8; MAX_CITY_NAME],\n    pub ref_count: i32,\n    pub edges: Vec<Edge>,\n    /// Kept explicitly so the `edge_count >= MAX_EDGES` check mirrors the C.\n    pub edge_count: i32,\n    /// `true` while the chunk is on one of the allocator's free lists, i.e.\n    /// between the `free()` in `delete_node` and the next `malloc()` that\n    /// hands the very same chunk out again.\n    pub freed: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Node {\n    /// The value of the `city_name` C string (bytes up to the first NUL).\n    pub fn name(&self) -> &[u8] {\n        match self.city_name.iter().position(|&b| b == 0) {\n            Some(i) => &self.city_name[..i],\n            None => &self.city_name[..],\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// glibc's `TCACHE_COUNT`: a size class caches at most 7 freed chunks.\nconst TCACHE_COUNT: usize = 7;\n\n/// What glibc's `free()` leaves in the first 16 bytes of the user data of a\n/// chunk it puts on a free list: `tcache_entry { next, key }` (or `fd`/`bk` for\n/// the unsorted bin).  Those 16 bytes overlap `node_t::city_name`, which is why\n/// a freed node's name turns into garbage and `strcmp()` in\n/// `get_node_by_name()`/`add_node()` stops matching it.  `ref_count` (offset\n/// 64) and `edges`/`edge_count` (offset 72 onwards) are *not* touched by\n/// `free()`, so they keep their values - the C program relies on that.\n///\n/// The real bytes are heap addresses (`next` is `PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`, i.e.\n/// `&next >> 12`), so they differ on every run because of ASLR; the placeholder\n/// below only has to have the same shape: non-empty, NUL-terminated inside the\n/// first 8 bytes and never equal to a city name a user could type.\nconst FREE_LIST_METADATA: [u8; 16] = [\n    0x0e, 0x8e, 0x38, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, // next = PROTECT_PTR(NULL)\n    0xa0, 0x92, 0x55, 0x55, 0x55, 0x55, 0x00, 0x00, // key  = &tcache\n];\n\n/// A maximal run of adjacent free chunks, `lo..=hi`, in address order - which\n/// is arena index order, because chunks are carved out of the top chunk from\n/// low to high.  glibc coalesces neighbouring free chunks, so its bins hold\n/// runs like this rather than individual chunks.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Run {\n    lo: NodeRef,\n    hi: NodeRef,\n}\n\n/// Backing store for every `malloc`ed `node_t`: one entry per distinct heap\n/// chunk, so a `NodeRef` behaves like the `node_t *` it stands for - including\n/// the fact that a chunk which is freed and allocated again yields the *same*\n/// pointer, which the C program's output exposes.\n///\n/// Every `node_t` lands in the same glibc size class (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240,\n/// so the chunk is 256 bytes), and this is what glibc 2.34 does with such a\n/// chunk - each rule below was checked against the C program:\n///\n///   * `free()` puts the chunk on the tcache bin as it is, while that bin holds\n///     fewer than `TCACHE_COUNT` chunks, so `malloc()` hands the most recently\n///     freed chunk back first;\n///   * with a full tcache bin the chunk is coalesced with the adjacent free\n///     runs (a chunk in the tcache still looks allocated and never takes part)\n///     and then either absorbed into the top chunk, if it borders it, or put at\n///     the head of the unsorted bin;\n///   * `malloc()` with an empty tcache bin empties the unsorted bin oldest\n///     first, moving same-sized chunks into the tcache and sorting bigger runs\n///     into their size bin, and then returns the *last* chunk it cached - so\n///     the newest of those chunks comes back first;\n///   * a run that is too big is taken from its bin (smallest first) and split,\n///     the caller gets its lowest chunk and the rest goes back to the unsorted\n///     bin;\n///   * `free()` writes free list metadata (`tcache_entry`, or `fd`/`bk`) over\n///     the first 16 bytes of the chunk's user data, i.e. over the start of\n///     `city_name` - but only for the chunk that actually goes on a list: a\n///     chunk absorbed into the top chunk, or one that is merged into the middle\n///     or the end of a run, keeps its name and stays findable by\n///     `get_node_by_name()`;\n///   * freeing a chunk that is already free is a double free: glibc prints a\n///     diagnostic and aborts instead of returning.\npub struct Arena {\n    pub nodes: Vec<Node>,\n    /// How many chunks are carved out of the top chunk.  Freeing the highest\n    /// chunk absorbs it back into the top chunk, which moves this down again;\n    /// `nodes[top..]` is the storage inside the top chunk, which the C program\n    /// can still read through a stale pointer.\n    top: usize,","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":643}}}
