{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019vcPjzY3gULZpz6Wjid4Z7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential findings: `c_src` vs. `translation`\n2\t\n3\tBoth programs are compared by running them:\n4\t\n5\t```sh\n6\tcd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver\n7\tcd translation && cargo build --release                                # -> translation/target/release/driver\n8\tcd translation && cargo test                                           # runs both and diffs them\n9\t```\n10\t\n11\t`tests/differential.rs` spawns both binaries, writes the same bytes to stdin and\n12\tcompares **stdout, stderr and the exit status** (exit code *and* terminating\n13\tsignal) for every input listed below.\n14\t\n15\tTo diff a single input by hand:\n16\t\n17\t```sh\n18\tprintf '1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n' > /tmp/in\n19\tdiff <(c_src/build/driver < /tmp/in) <(translation/target/release/driver < /tmp/in)\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tOn top of the cases below, 1200 randomly generated sessions (mixed and\n23\tdelete-heavy, city names including the empty one, over-long ones and ones that\n24\tlook like menu choices; distances including negative, `INT_MAX` and unparsable\n25\tones) were compared the same way. Every one of them either matched exactly or\n26\tfell into the class of section 3, which is detected by re-running the C program\n27\tand noticing that it disagrees with itself. That harness is kept as\n28\t\n29\t```sh\n30\tcd translation && python3 tools/fuzz.py <seed> <rounds>\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tIt is not part of `cargo test`, because it would report the inherently\n34\tirreproducible inputs of section 3 as differences on some runs.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 1. Mismatches that were found and fixed\n39\t\n40\t### 1.1 A freed node was still found by name\n41\t\n42\t*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 7 A / 8`, `1 A / 7 A / 4 A / 8`,\n43\t`1 A / 1 B / 7 B / 2 A B 5 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 5 A B / 8`,\n44\t`1 A / 7 A / 6 A / 8`\n45\t\n46\t* C: `City 'A' not found`\n47\t* Rust (before): `Current ref count: 0` / `City: A ...` — the node was still\n48\t  found.\n49\t\n50\t*Cause:* `delete_node()` `free()`s the node while `graph->nodes[]` keeps the\n51\tpointer, and `get_node_by_name()`/`add_node()` then `strcmp()` freed memory.\n52\tglibc's `free()` writes `tcache_entry { next, key }` over the **first 16 bytes**\n53\tof the chunk's user data, and those 16 bytes are the start of\n54\t`node_t::city_name`, so the name turns into garbage and never compares equal\n55\tagain. `ref_count` (offset 64) and `edges`/`edge_count` (offset 72 onwards) are\n56\t*not* touched, which is why the C program keeps printing `ref_count: 0` and\n57\tkeeps following the freed node's edges.\n58\t\n59\t*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` (in `src/dag_lib.rs`) overwrites the first 16 bytes\n60\tof `city_name` with a free-list-metadata placeholder (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`) and\n61\tleaves everything else alone.\n62\t\n63\t### 1.1b ... but not every freed node loses its name\n64\t\n65\t*Input:* ten cities, `7` on seven of them (which fills the tcache bin), then\n66\t\n67\t* `7 N10 / 4 N10` — N10 is the highest chunk, so `free()` absorbs it into the\n68\t  top chunk and rewrites only the chunk *header*: C prints\n69\t  `City: N10 (ref_count: 0)`.\n70\t* `7 N8 / 7 N9 / 4 N9 / 4 N8` — N9 is merged into the run that starts at N8, so\n71\t  `fd`/`bk` land in N8's user data only: C finds N9 (`ref_count: 0`) but not N8.\n72\t\n73\tRust (before the fix) reported \"not found\" in both cases, because every freed\n74\tchunk was garbled.\n75\t\n76\t*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` writes the metadata only into the chunk that really\n77\tgoes on a list — the chunk itself for the tcache, the *lowest* chunk of the\n78\tcoalesced run for the unsorted bin, nothing at all when the run is absorbed into\n79\tthe top chunk — and `malloc_node()` garbles the remainder's lowest chunk when it\n80\tsplits a run. Covered by `some_freed_nodes_keep_their_name`.\n81\t\n82\t### 1.2 A deleted city could not be added again, and reuse of the chunk was not modelled\n83\t\n84\t*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 1 A / 3 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 7 A / 7 B / 1 C / 1 D / 3 / 8`\n85\t\n86\t* C: the duplicate check does not fire (see 1.1), the city is added a second\n87\t  time, and `malloc()` hands back the **chunk that was just freed** — so\n88\t  `graph->nodes[0]` and `graph->nodes[1]` are the same pointer and\n89\t  `print_graph` prints the same city twice. With two frees and two adds the\n90\t  names come back swapped (`D C C D`), because the tcache is LIFO.\n91\t* Rust (before): `Error: Node 'A' already exists` / `Failed to add city`, and\n92\t  every `add_node` created a brand new arena slot.\n93\t\n94\t*Cause:* the arena never reused a slot, so a `NodeRef` could not alias the way a\n95\trecycled `node_t *` does.\n96\t\n97\t*Fix:* `Arena` now models glibc 2.34's behaviour for the single size class every\n98\t`node_t` falls into (`sizeof(node_t)` = 240, so a 256-byte chunk):\n99\t\n100\t* `free()` pushes the chunk on the tcache bin (LIFO) while it holds fewer than\n101\t  `TCACHE_COUNT` = 7 chunks, so `malloc()` returns the most recently freed chunk;\n102\t* with a full tcache bin the chunk is coalesced with the adjacent free runs (a\n103\t  chunk sitting in the tcache still looks allocated and never coalesces) and is\n104\t  then either absorbed into the top chunk, if it borders it, or appended to the\n105\t  unsorted bin;\n106\t* `malloc()` with an empty tcache bin empties the unsorted bin oldest first,\n107\t  moving equally sized chunks into the tcache and sorting longer runs into their\n108\t  size bin, and returns the last chunk it cached — so those chunks come back\n109\t  **newest freed first**;\n110\t* a run that is longer than one chunk is taken from its bin (shortest first) and\n111\t  split: the caller gets its lowest chunk, the rest goes back to the unsorted\n112\t  bin;\n113\t* chunks that are still inside the top chunk are carved off again in ascending\n114\t  address order.\n115\t\n116\tEvery rule was checked against the C program: 9 nodes freed ascending, 9 freed\n117\tdescending, 16 freed and re-added, interleaved free/alloc, and two isolated\n118\toverflow chunks freed in either order — see\n119\t`freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first`,\n120\t`freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit` and\n121\t`overflowing_the_tcache_hands_chunks_back_newest_first`.\n122\t\n123\t### 1.2b Reuse order beyond the tcache limit\n124\t\n125\t*Input:* twelve cities, `7` on seven of them, then `7 N9 / 7 N11` (or\n126\t`7 N11 / 7 N9`), then nine more `1` commands and `3`.\n127\t\n128\tAn intermediate version of the fix handed overflow chunks back in ascending\n129\taddress order, which matches when they get coalesced but not when they are\n130\tisolated: C reuses N11 before N9 when N11 was freed *last*, because `malloc`\n131\tmoves the whole unsorted bin into the tcache oldest first and then pops it.\n132\tFixed by modelling the unsorted-bin walk itself (see above).\n133\t\n134\t### 1.3 A double free must kill the process\n135\t\n136\t*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 6 A / 8`\n137\t\n138\t* C: `stderr` = `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, killed by SIGABRT\n139\t  (status 134), and **stdout is empty** because the buffered output was never\n140\t  flushed.\n141\t* Rust (before): exited 0 after printing everything.\n142\t\n143\t*Cause:* `7 B` frees B (`ref_count` 1 → 0). `shallow_copy(A)` walks A's edges\n144\tand revives the freed B (`ref_count` 0 → 1) — a write to freed memory that\n145\tglibc does not notice because `ref_count` is outside the free-list metadata.\n146\t`free_graph()` at exit then decrements B to 0 and frees it a second time.\n147\t\n148\t*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` detects a free of a chunk that is already on a free\n149\tlist and calls `cio::malloc_printerr()`, which writes glibc's diagnostic to fd 2\n150\tand `abort()`s. Because `COut` keeps its own 4096-byte buffer and `abort()`\n151\tneither flushes nor runs destructors, the buffered stdout is discarded exactly\n152\tas in C.\n153\t\n154\tThe message depends on *where* the chunk sits, and all three variants the\n155\tprogram can produce are reproduced (`Arena::report_double_free`):\n156\t\n157\t| situation | stderr |\n158\t|---|---|\n159\t| chunk in the tcache bin | `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` |\n160\t| chunk absorbed into the top chunk | `double free or corruption (out)` |\n161\t| chunk on the unsorted bin / a size bin | `double free or corruption (!prev)` |\n162\t\n163\tThe last two are reached by filling the tcache with seven frees first (see\n164\t`double_free_aborts_the_process`); there the C program has already written one\n165\tfull 4096-byte block, so stdout is 4096 bytes rather than empty.\n166\t\n167\t### 1.4 An overflowing distance sum can make the `previous` chain cyclic\n168\t\n169\t*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 1 C / 2 A B 2000000000 / 2 B C 2000000000 /\n170\t2 B A 2000000000 / 5 A C / 8`\n171\t\n172\t* C: dies from a signal (SIGSEGV, occasionally SIGBUS) with empty stderr and\n173\t  only the already-written 4096-byte stdout blocks.\n174\t* Rust (before): looped for ever, growing a `Vec` (killed by `timeout`).\n175\t\n176\t*Cause:* `find_shortest_path()` relaxes *every* neighbour, including nodes that\n177\tare already `visited`. With non-negative weights that never lowers a finalised\n178\tdistance — but `state[i].distance + edge->distance` overflows `int` here and\n179\tbecomes negative, so an already visited node gets a new `previous`. That makes\n180\tthe `previous` chain cyclic (`A->B->A`), and the reconstruction loop\n181\t`path[count++] = current_node` runs for ever past the end of the\n182\t`node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` **stack** array until the smashed frame kills the\n183\tprocess.\n184\t\n185\t*Fix:* the reconstruction loop in `find_shortest_path()` calls\n186\t`cio::stack_smash()` when it is about to write entry number `MAX_NODES + 1`\n187\t(a chain longer than `MAX_NODES` can only exist if it contains a cycle, since\n188\tthere are at most `MAX_NODES` distinct chunks). `stack_smash()` restores the\n189\tdefault `SIGSEGV` disposition — the Rust runtime installs a handler for stack\n190\toverflow reporting that would otherwise swallow it — and raises `SIGSEGV`\n191\twithout flushing stdout.\n192\t\n193\tA legitimate path of exactly `MAX_NODES` nodes must still be printed, which the\n194\t`100 city chain` case in `hundred_node_graph` pins down.\n195\t\n196\t---\n197\t\n198\t## 2. Behaviour that was already correct and is now covered by tests\n199\t\n200\tThese were verified rather than fixed, but they are the parts most likely to\n201\tdrift, so they are listed for the next reader:\n202\t\n203\t* `fgets(buf, 256, stdin)` stops after 255 bytes, so an over-long line is split\n204\t  and its tail is read as the *next* answer; `sscanf(\"%d\")` in contrast skips\n205\t  leading whitespace, accepts a sign and ignores trailing junk.\n206\t* glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol` (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) and\n207\t  then *truncates* to `int`: `2147483648` becomes `-2147483648` (rejected as a\n208\t  negative distance), `4294967304` becomes `8` (a valid menu choice) and\n209\t  `99999999999999999999` becomes `-1`.\n210\t* `strncpy(name, city, 63)` + `name[63] = 0` truncates a city name to 63 bytes,\n211\t  while the duplicate check compares against the *untruncated* input — so two\n212\t  names that differ only after byte 63 both get added.\n213\t* `input[strcspn(input, \"\\n\")] = 0` also stops at a NUL, so a name containing a\n214\t  NUL byte is cut there; names are raw bytes and may be invalid UTF-8.\n215\t* An edge of length `INT_MAX` is indistinguishable from \"unreachable\", because\n216\t  `INT_MAX` is the sentinel the algorithm starts from and the relaxation uses a\n217\t  strict `<`.\n218\t* Ties in \"next unvisited node with minimum distance\" go to the **first**\n219\t  candidate found (`<`, not `<=`).\n220\t* `EOF` at any sub-prompt only breaks out of the `switch`; the menu is printed\n221\t  once more before the outer loop ends.\n222\t* stdout is fully buffered (4096-byte blocks) and stderr is unbuffered, which is\n223\t  what makes stdout empty vs. 4096/8192 bytes long in the crash cases above.\n224\t* `print_graph()` iterates `graph->nodes[]`, so a node that was deleted (and a\n225\t  node that was added twice) is printed once per entry.\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t## 3. Inputs whose C behaviour is not reproducible\n230\t\n231\t### 3.1 Printing a node whose name was overwritten by free list metadata\n232\t\n233\t*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 3 / 8` (and any other input that prints such a node, e.g.\n234\t`print_node` following an edge into one).\n235\t\n236\tThis is only about the chunks that really received the metadata (see 1.1b — a\n237\tnode absorbed into the top chunk or merged into a run keeps its name and is\n238\tcompared byte for byte like any other). `city_name` then contains glibc's\n239\t`tcache_entry::next`, which is `PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`, i.e. `&next >> 12` — a\n240\t**heap address**. With ASLR the C program prints different bytes on every run:\n241\t\n242\t```\n243\tCity: \\x0e\\xc8\\x03 (ref_count: 0)\n244\tCity: \\xaa\\x60 (ref_count: 0)\n245\tCity: C\\xce\\x02 (ref_count: 0)\n246\t```\n247\t\n248\tNo translation can match that byte for byte. The Rust program produces garbage\n249\tof the same shape (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`, NUL-terminated inside the first 8\n250\tbytes) so that everything *around* it — the `ref_count: 0`, the `Edges:` list\n251\tand every later lookup — still matches.\n252\t\n253\t`printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c` asserts this explicitly: it\n254\truns the C program six times, requires that it disagrees with itself, and then\n255\tchecks that stderr, the exit status, the output before the name and the whole\n256\ttail after it are identical to the Rust program's. That test fails (rather than\n257\tsilently passing) if the C output ever becomes deterministic here.\n258\t\n259\t### 3.2 Which signal the smashed stack produces (see 1.4)\n260\t\n261\tstdout, stderr and \"died from a signal\" are stable, but the C program dies from\n262\tSIGSEGV in roughly four runs out of five and from SIGBUS otherwise, depending on\n263\twhat the runaway writes clobber first. The Rust program always raises SIGSEGV,\n264\tthe common case; `distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic` accepts\n265\teither signal from the C side and requires identical output.\n266\t\n267\t---\n268\t\n269\t## 4. Branches that no input can reach in either program\n270\t\n271\tThe C contains 57 distinct `printf`/`fprintf` format strings. Every fixed piece\n272\tof 48 of them appears verbatim in the Rust sources; the nine that do not are\n273\texactly the ones listed below, which nothing can reach.\n274\t\n275\tRecorded so the absence of tests is not mistaken for a gap:\n276\t\n277\t* every `if (!ptr)` guard in `lib.c` (`add_node`, `add_edge`, `shallow_copy`,\n278\t  `find_shortest_path`, `get_node_by_name`, `delete_node`, `free_graph`) —\n279\t  `main()` never passes a null pointer, so the `Error: NULL ... ` messages are\n280\t  dead code. The translation encodes non-nullness in its types instead.\n281\t* `printf(\"NULL node\\n\")` / `printf(\"NULL graph\\n\")` in `print_node`/\n282\t  `print_graph`, for the same reason.\n283\t* every `malloc` failure path (`Failed to create graph`, `Error: Failed to\n284\t  allocate node/graph/path`) — not reachable without an allocation failure.\n285\t* `shallow_copy()` returning `NULL`, hence `Failed to create shallow copy` in\n286\t  case 6.\n287\t* `current_idx == -1` in the Dijkstra loop and `current_state_idx == -1` in the\n288\t  reconstruction loop: both look the node up in the array it was taken from.\n289\t* the `state_count < MAX_NODES` limit on adding a neighbour, and the\n290\t  `*visited_count < MAX_NODES` limit in `increment_refs_recursive`: `add_node`\n291\t  caps the graph at `MAX_NODES` nodes, so neither array can overflow.\n292\t\n293\t## 5. The one allocation the arena deliberately does not model\n294\t\n295\t`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t *) * count` for its result and\n296\t`main` frees it again right after printing. For `count` 30 or 31 that request\n297\tlands in the **same size class as `node_t`** (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240, so both\n298\tround up to a 256-byte chunk), so such a path can take a chunk off the node free\n299\tlist. The arena does not model this allocation, because it cannot be observed:\n300\t\n301\t* `malloc` then `free` with no `add_city` in between is LIFO, so a chunk taken\n302\t  from the tcache goes straight back on top of it;\n303\t* with an empty tcache the chunk comes from the lowest overflow chunk (or from\n304\t  the top) and is pushed onto the empty tcache, which is exactly the chunk the\n305\t  next `add_city` would have been given anyway;\n306\t* the resulting addresses are only observable through pointer *identity*, which\n307\t  a `NodeRef` reproduces.\n308\t\n309\tTwo sessions that force this — a 30-city path taken with a drained tcache and\n310\tnine chunks on the overflow list, and the same with a full tcache — are part of\n311\t`freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation` and match byte for byte.\n312\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"4881c985-64d2-4ae1-b046-7e1ba53ea6fa","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:13:32.349Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# Differential findings: `c_src` vs. `translation`\n\nBoth programs are compared by running them:\n\n```sh\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver\ncd translation && cargo build --release                                # -> translation/target/release/driver\ncd translation && cargo test                                           # runs both and diffs them\n```\n\n`tests/differential.rs` spawns both binaries, writes the same bytes to stdin and\ncompares **stdout, stderr and the exit status** (exit code *and* terminating\nsignal) for every input listed below.\n\nTo diff a single input by hand:\n\n```sh\nprintf '1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n' > /tmp/in\ndiff <(c_src/build/driver < /tmp/in) <(translation/target/release/driver < /tmp/in)\n```\n\nOn top of the cases below, 1200 randomly generated sessions (mixed and\ndelete-heavy, city names including the empty one, over-long ones and ones that\nlook like menu choices; distances including negative, `INT_MAX` and unparsable\nones) were compared the same way. Every one of them either matched exactly or\nfell into the class of section 3, which is detected by re-running the C program\nand noticing that it disagrees with itself. That harness is kept as\n\n```sh\ncd translation && python3 tools/fuzz.py <seed> <rounds>\n```\n\nIt is not part of `cargo test`, because it would report the inherently\nirreproducible inputs of section 3 as differences on some runs.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mismatches that were found and fixed\n\n### 1.1 A freed node was still found by name\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 7 A / 8`, `1 A / 7 A / 4 A / 8`,\n`1 A / 1 B / 7 B / 2 A B 5 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 5 A B / 8`,\n`1 A / 7 A / 6 A / 8`\n\n* C: `City 'A' not found`\n* Rust (before): `Current ref count: 0` / `City: A ...` — the node was still\n  found.\n\n*Cause:* `delete_node()` `free()`s the node while `graph->nodes[]` keeps the\npointer, and `get_node_by_name()`/`add_node()` then `strcmp()` freed memory.\nglibc's `free()` writes `tcache_entry { next, key }` over the **first 16 bytes**\nof the chunk's user data, and those 16 bytes are the start of\n`node_t::city_name`, so the name turns into garbage and never compares equal\nagain. `ref_count` (offset 64) and `edges`/`edge_count` (offset 72 onwards) are\n*not* touched, which is why the C program keeps printing `ref_count: 0` and\nkeeps following the freed node's edges.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` (in `src/dag_lib.rs`) overwrites the first 16 bytes\nof `city_name` with a free-list-metadata placeholder (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`) and\nleaves everything else alone.\n\n### 1.1b ... but not every freed node loses its name\n\n*Input:* ten cities, `7` on seven of them (which fills the tcache bin), then\n\n* `7 N10 / 4 N10` — N10 is the highest chunk, so `free()` absorbs it into the\n  top chunk and rewrites only the chunk *header*: C prints\n  `City: N10 (ref_count: 0)`.\n* `7 N8 / 7 N9 / 4 N9 / 4 N8` — N9 is merged into the run that starts at N8, so\n  `fd`/`bk` land in N8's user data only: C finds N9 (`ref_count: 0`) but not N8.\n\nRust (before the fix) reported \"not found\" in both cases, because every freed\nchunk was garbled.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` writes the metadata only into the chunk that really\ngoes on a list — the chunk itself for the tcache, the *lowest* chunk of the\ncoalesced run for the unsorted bin, nothing at all when the run is absorbed into\nthe top chunk — and `malloc_node()` garbles the remainder's lowest chunk when it\nsplits a run. Covered by `some_freed_nodes_keep_their_name`.\n\n### 1.2 A deleted city could not be added again, and reuse of the chunk was not modelled\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 1 A / 3 / 8`, `1 A / 1 B / 7 A / 7 B / 1 C / 1 D / 3 / 8`\n\n* C: the duplicate check does not fire (see 1.1), the city is added a second\n  time, and `malloc()` hands back the **chunk that was just freed** — so\n  `graph->nodes[0]` and `graph->nodes[1]` are the same pointer and\n  `print_graph` prints the same city twice. With two frees and two adds the\n  names come back swapped (`D C C D`), because the tcache is LIFO.\n* Rust (before): `Error: Node 'A' already exists` / `Failed to add city`, and\n  every `add_node` created a brand new arena slot.\n\n*Cause:* the arena never reused a slot, so a `NodeRef` could not alias the way a\nrecycled `node_t *` does.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena` now models glibc 2.34's behaviour for the single size class every\n`node_t` falls into (`sizeof(node_t)` = 240, so a 256-byte chunk):\n\n* `free()` pushes the chunk on the tcache bin (LIFO) while it holds fewer than\n  `TCACHE_COUNT` = 7 chunks, so `malloc()` returns the most recently freed chunk;\n* with a full tcache bin the chunk is coalesced with the adjacent free runs (a\n  chunk sitting in the tcache still looks allocated and never coalesces) and is\n  then either absorbed into the top chunk, if it borders it, or appended to the\n  unsorted bin;\n* `malloc()` with an empty tcache bin empties the unsorted bin oldest first,\n  moving equally sized chunks into the tcache and sorting longer runs into their\n  size bin, and returns the last chunk it cached — so those chunks come back\n  **newest freed first**;\n* a run that is longer than one chunk is taken from its bin (shortest first) and\n  split: the caller gets its lowest chunk, the rest goes back to the unsorted\n  bin;\n* chunks that are still inside the top chunk are carved off again in ascending\n  address order.\n\nEvery rule was checked against the C program: 9 nodes freed ascending, 9 freed\ndescending, 16 freed and re-added, interleaved free/alloc, and two isolated\noverflow chunks freed in either order — see\n`freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first`,\n`freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit` and\n`overflowing_the_tcache_hands_chunks_back_newest_first`.\n\n### 1.2b Reuse order beyond the tcache limit\n\n*Input:* twelve cities, `7` on seven of them, then `7 N9 / 7 N11` (or\n`7 N11 / 7 N9`), then nine more `1` commands and `3`.\n\nAn intermediate version of the fix handed overflow chunks back in ascending\naddress order, which matches when they get coalesced but not when they are\nisolated: C reuses N11 before N9 when N11 was freed *last*, because `malloc`\nmoves the whole unsorted bin into the tcache oldest first and then pops it.\nFixed by modelling the unsorted-bin walk itself (see above).\n\n### 1.3 A double free must kill the process\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 5 / 7 B / 6 A / 8`\n\n* C: `stderr` = `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, killed by SIGABRT\n  (status 134), and **stdout is empty** because the buffered output was never\n  flushed.\n* Rust (before): exited 0 after printing everything.\n\n*Cause:* `7 B` frees B (`ref_count` 1 → 0). `shallow_copy(A)` walks A's edges\nand revives the freed B (`ref_count` 0 → 1) — a write to freed memory that\nglibc does not notice because `ref_count` is outside the free-list metadata.\n`free_graph()` at exit then decrements B to 0 and frees it a second time.\n\n*Fix:* `Arena::free_node()` detects a free of a chunk that is already on a free\nlist and calls `cio::malloc_printerr()`, which writes glibc's diagnostic to fd 2\nand `abort()`s. Because `COut` keeps its own 4096-byte buffer and `abort()`\nneither flushes nor runs destructors, the buffered stdout is discarded exactly\nas in C.\n\nThe message depends on *where* the chunk sits, and all three variants the\nprogram can produce are reproduced (`Arena::report_double_free`):\n\n| situation | stderr |\n|---|---|\n| chunk in the tcache bin | `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` |\n| chunk absorbed into the top chunk | `double free or corruption (out)` |\n| chunk on the unsorted bin / a size bin | `double free or corruption (!prev)` |\n\nThe last two are reached by filling the tcache with seven frees first (see\n`double_free_aborts_the_process`); there the C program has already written one\nfull 4096-byte block, so stdout is 4096 bytes rather than empty.\n\n### 1.4 An overflowing distance sum can make the `previous` chain cyclic\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 1 B / 1 C / 2 A B 2000000000 / 2 B C 2000000000 /\n2 B A 2000000000 / 5 A C / 8`\n\n* C: dies from a signal (SIGSEGV, occasionally SIGBUS) with empty stderr and\n  only the already-written 4096-byte stdout blocks.\n* Rust (before): looped for ever, growing a `Vec` (killed by `timeout`).\n\n*Cause:* `find_shortest_path()` relaxes *every* neighbour, including nodes that\nare already `visited`. With non-negative weights that never lowers a finalised\ndistance — but `state[i].distance + edge->distance` overflows `int` here and\nbecomes negative, so an already visited node gets a new `previous`. That makes\nthe `previous` chain cyclic (`A->B->A`), and the reconstruction loop\n`path[count++] = current_node` runs for ever past the end of the\n`node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` **stack** array until the smashed frame kills the\nprocess.\n\n*Fix:* the reconstruction loop in `find_shortest_path()` calls\n`cio::stack_smash()` when it is about to write entry number `MAX_NODES + 1`\n(a chain longer than `MAX_NODES` can only exist if it contains a cycle, since\nthere are at most `MAX_NODES` distinct chunks). `stack_smash()` restores the\ndefault `SIGSEGV` disposition — the Rust runtime installs a handler for stack\noverflow reporting that would otherwise swallow it — and raises `SIGSEGV`\nwithout flushing stdout.\n\nA legitimate path of exactly `MAX_NODES` nodes must still be printed, which the\n`100 city chain` case in `hundred_node_graph` pins down.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Behaviour that was already correct and is now covered by tests\n\nThese were verified rather than fixed, but they are the parts most likely to\ndrift, so they are listed for the next reader:\n\n* `fgets(buf, 256, stdin)` stops after 255 bytes, so an over-long line is split\n  and its tail is read as the *next* answer; `sscanf(\"%d\")` in contrast skips\n  leading whitespace, accepts a sign and ignores trailing junk.\n* glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol` (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) and\n  then *truncates* to `int`: `2147483648` becomes `-2147483648` (rejected as a\n  negative distance), `4294967304` becomes `8` (a valid menu choice) and\n  `99999999999999999999` becomes `-1`.\n* `strncpy(name, city, 63)` + `name[63] = 0` truncates a city name to 63 bytes,\n  while the duplicate check compares against the *untruncated* input — so two\n  names that differ only after byte 63 both get added.\n* `input[strcspn(input, \"\\n\")] = 0` also stops at a NUL, so a name containing a\n  NUL byte is cut there; names are raw bytes and may be invalid UTF-8.\n* An edge of length `INT_MAX` is indistinguishable from \"unreachable\", because\n  `INT_MAX` is the sentinel the algorithm starts from and the relaxation uses a\n  strict `<`.\n* Ties in \"next unvisited node with minimum distance\" go to the **first**\n  candidate found (`<`, not `<=`).\n* `EOF` at any sub-prompt only breaks out of the `switch`; the menu is printed\n  once more before the outer loop ends.\n* stdout is fully buffered (4096-byte blocks) and stderr is unbuffered, which is\n  what makes stdout empty vs. 4096/8192 bytes long in the crash cases above.\n* `print_graph()` iterates `graph->nodes[]`, so a node that was deleted (and a\n  node that was added twice) is printed once per entry.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Inputs whose C behaviour is not reproducible\n\n### 3.1 Printing a node whose name was overwritten by free list metadata\n\n*Input:* `1 A / 7 A / 3 / 8` (and any other input that prints such a node, e.g.\n`print_node` following an edge into one).\n\nThis is only about the chunks that really received the metadata (see 1.1b — a\nnode absorbed into the top chunk or merged into a run keeps its name and is\ncompared byte for byte like any other). `city_name` then contains glibc's\n`tcache_entry::next`, which is `PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`, i.e. `&next >> 12` — a\n**heap address**. With ASLR the C program prints different bytes on every run:\n\n```\nCity: \\x0e\\xc8\\x03 (ref_count: 0)\nCity: \\xaa\\x60 (ref_count: 0)\nCity: C\\xce\\x02 (ref_count: 0)\n```\n\nNo translation can match that byte for byte. The Rust program produces garbage\nof the same shape (`FREE_LIST_METADATA`, NUL-terminated inside the first 8\nbytes) so that everything *around* it — the `ref_count: 0`, the `Edges:` list\nand every later lookup — still matches.\n\n`printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c` asserts this explicitly: it\nruns the C program six times, requires that it disagrees with itself, and then\nchecks that stderr, the exit status, the output before the name and the whole\ntail after it are identical to the Rust program's. That test fails (rather than\nsilently passing) if the C output ever becomes deterministic here.\n\n### 3.2 Which signal the smashed stack produces (see 1.4)\n\nstdout, stderr and \"died from a signal\" are stable, but the C program dies from\nSIGSEGV in roughly four runs out of five and from SIGBUS otherwise, depending on\nwhat the runaway writes clobber first. The Rust program always raises SIGSEGV,\nthe common case; `distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic` accepts\neither signal from the C side and requires identical output.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Branches that no input can reach in either program\n\nThe C contains 57 distinct `printf`/`fprintf` format strings. Every fixed piece\nof 48 of them appears verbatim in the Rust sources; the nine that do not are\nexactly the ones listed below, which nothing can reach.\n\nRecorded so the absence of tests is not mistaken for a gap:\n\n* every `if (!ptr)` guard in `lib.c` (`add_node`, `add_edge`, `shallow_copy`,\n  `find_shortest_path`, `get_node_by_name`, `delete_node`, `free_graph`) —\n  `main()` never passes a null pointer, so the `Error: NULL ... ` messages are\n  dead code. The translation encodes non-nullness in its types instead.\n* `printf(\"NULL node\\n\")` / `printf(\"NULL graph\\n\")` in `print_node`/\n  `print_graph`, for the same reason.\n* every `malloc` failure path (`Failed to create graph`, `Error: Failed to\n  allocate node/graph/path`) — not reachable without an allocation failure.\n* `shallow_copy()` returning `NULL`, hence `Failed to create shallow copy` in\n  case 6.\n* `current_idx == -1` in the Dijkstra loop and `current_state_idx == -1` in the\n  reconstruction loop: both look the node up in the array it was taken from.\n* the `state_count < MAX_NODES` limit on adding a neighbour, and the\n  `*visited_count < MAX_NODES` limit in `increment_refs_recursive`: `add_node`\n  caps the graph at `MAX_NODES` nodes, so neither array can overflow.\n\n## 5. The one allocation the arena deliberately does not model\n\n`find_shortest_path` allocates `sizeof(node_t *) * count` for its result and\n`main` frees it again right after printing. For `count` 30 or 31 that request\nlands in the **same size class as `node_t`** (`sizeof(node_t)` is 240, so both\nround up to a 256-byte chunk), so such a path can take a chunk off the node free\nlist. The arena does not model this allocation, because it cannot be observed:\n\n* `malloc` then `free` with no `add_city` in between is LIFO, so a chunk taken\n  from the tcache goes straight back on top of it;\n* with an empty tcache the chunk comes from the lowest overflow chunk (or from\n  the top) and is pushed onto the empty tcache, which is exactly the chunk the\n  next `add_city` would have been given anyway;\n* the resulting addresses are only observable through pointer *identity*, which\n  a `NodeRef` reproduces.\n\nTwo sessions that force this — a 30-city path taken with a drained tcache and\nnine chunks on the overflow list, and the same with a full tcache — are part of\n`freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation` and match byte for byte.\n","numLines":312,"startLine":1,"totalLines":312}}}
