{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae61d349e07c0e8cb","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Xf9DcA6XRycmMGLnneRC78","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-FO9Zfv/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-FbQLo9/c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752/tasks/ae61d349e07c0e8cb.output","summary":"## Audit result\n\nI read both implementations line by line and then ran ~4,500 differential inputs (running the C with ASLR disabled so it becomes deterministic, and normalising only the inherently-nondeterministic freed-chunk garbage bytes). **I found exactly one root-cause divergence.** Everything else matched byte for byte.\n\n---\n\n### 1. CONFIRMED BUG — `find_shortest_path` kills the process where the C survives and prints 101 path entries\n\n**C** — `c_src/src/lib.c:283-304`:\n```c\nnode_t *path[MAX_NODES];\nint count = 0;\nnode_t *current_node = end;\nwhile (current_node) {\n    path[count++] = current_node;          // line 288 - NO bound check\n    ...\n    current_node = state[current_state_idx].previous;\n}\n```\n**Rust** — `translation/src/dag_lib.rs:562-571` (helper at `translation/src/cio.rs:139-156`):\n```rust\nwhile let Some(cn) = current_node {\n    if path.len() == MAX_NODES {\n        crate::cio::stack_smash();         // line 569 -> raise(SIGSEGV)\n    }\n    path.push(cn);\n```\n\n**Exact stdin that exposes it** (4 cities; the `2147483647` edge makes `state[C].distance + 2147483647` overflow, so the already-visited `B` gets `previous = C` while `C.previous = B` — a cycle in the `previous` chain):\n\n```\n1\nA\n1\nB\n1\nC\n1\nD\n2\nA\nB\n1\n2\nB\nC\n1\n2\nC\nB\n2147483647\n2\nC\nD\n1\n5\nA\nD\n8\n```\ni.e. `printf '1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nB\\n2147483647\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n'`\n\n**Confirmed by running both:**\n\n| | exit status | stdout | stderr |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C | **0** | **3400 bytes**, `Shortest path from A to D:` followed by **101** entries `1. B / 2. C / 3. B / … / 100. C / 101. D` | empty |\n| Rust | **139 (SIGSEGV)** | **0 bytes** | empty |\n\nDeterministic: 15/15 C runs give status 0 / 3400 bytes (both with and without ASLR); 15/15 Rust runs give SIGSEGV / 0 bytes.\n\n**Why the C survives.** The OOB write `path[100]` aliases `state[0].node` in the stack frame. It stores `current_node` there, so the very next lookup finds it at index 0 and reads `state[0].previous`, which is `NULL` for the start node — the loop exits with `count == 101`. `main.c:194` then prints 101 entries and the program continues normally. The Rust's comment (\"the C program then writes past the array for ever\") is only true when the *start* node also acquired a `previous`, which needs a second overflowing edge pointing back into the start.\n\n**Scope — this is not a rare corner.** A systematic sweep of \"chain of k nodes + one overflowing back-edge\" (`translation`-vs-`c_src`, 20 shapes) gave:\n\n| shape | C | Rust |\n|---|---|---|\n| back-edge to a non-start node (15 of 20 shapes) | exit 0, 101 entries printed | SIGSEGV, stdout lost |\n| back-edge to the start node, k=4,5,6 | exit 0, 107/111/110 entries | SIGSEGV |\n| back-edge to the start node, k=3,7 | dies (SIGSEGV / SIGBUS) | SIGSEGV (matches, modulo signal) |\n\nA random fuzz run biased toward large distances (250 sessions, 6 cities) produced 38 divergences, all from this one cause: 13 × `C exit 0 / R SIGSEGV`, 24 × `C SIGBUS / R SIGSEGV`, and 1 × **`C SIGABRT with stderr \"free(): double free detected in tcache 2\" / R SIGSEGV with empty stderr`** — i.e. the premature crash also swallows later, otherwise-correct behaviour (the C session went on to hit a double free; the Rust never got there).\n\nA minimal fix direction: instead of crashing at 100, emulate the aliasing — the 101st write goes to `state[0].node`, the 102nd to `state[0].distance`, the 103rd to `state[0].previous`, the 104th to `state[0].visited`, the 105th to `state[1].node`, … and only die once the runaway writes leave the frame.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Areas where I found NO difference (stated explicitly)\n\n* **Every format string** (`c_src/src/main.c:34-258`, `c_src/src/lib.c:36-360` vs `translation/src/main.rs:15-313`, `translation/src/dag_lib.rs:346-628`) — compared character by character including the two-space `\"  Edges:\\n\"`, the four-space `\"    -> %s (distance: %d)\\n\"`, `\"  %d. %s\\n\"`, the `'…'` quoting in `City '%s' not found`, and `Freeing graph and exiting...`. All identical. `%d` vs `%s` usage identical. No difference.\n* **Order of validation/error checks.** `add_node` (full → duplicate), `add_edge` (max-edges → negative distance → duplicate edge), `find_shortest_path` (`end_idx == -1 || distance == INT_MAX`), and each menu case (case 2 validates the distance *before* looking up the cities; `!from` is reported before `!to`) — all match. No difference.\n* **Loop bounds / off-by-one / index choice.** `i + 1` in the path print, `result[i] = path[count - 1 - i]`, `0..edge_count`, `0..node_count`, `0..state.len()`, tie-breaking with strict `<` (first minimum wins) — all match. Verified with a legitimate 100-node chain (`chain100`: exact `MAX_NODES` path, matched byte for byte).\n* **Integer types / truncation / overflow.** `cio::sscanf_int` reproduces glibc's `strtol`-then-truncate-to-`int`: `4294967297` → `1` (Add city), `4294967304` → `8` (Exit), `2147483648` → `-2147483648`, `99999999999999999999` → `-1`, `+8`, `08`, `8abc`, `0x8`, `.5`, `-`, empty, leading whitespace — all 26 hand cases matched. `wrapping_add` in the relaxation matches gcc's wraparound. No difference.\n* **`break` inside a `switch` vs labelled blocks.** EOF injected at every sub-prompt of cases 1, 2 (×3), 4, 5 (×2), 6, 7, plus immediate EOF and no-trailing-newline: in every case both print the menu once more and then exit 0. No difference.\n* **stdout vs stderr routing.** All `Error: …` / `No path found` from `lib.c` go to stderr in both; all `main.c` messages to stdout in both; `case 5` correctly emits `No path found` to *both* streams. The 4096-byte full-buffering model (`cio::COut`) reproduces the C's write boundaries, including stdout truncation on `abort()` — verified via pipe and via file redirect (`st_blksize` here is 4096). No difference.\n* **Rust panic surfaces.** `edge_count` and `edges.len()` are always in sync (`alloc_node` resets both, `add_edge` pushes and increments together), `graph.nodes.len() == node_count`, `end_idx`/`neighbor_idx`/`current_state_idx` are all guarded before use, `.expect()` in `malloc_node` is guarded by `cached`, and the release profile has no overflow checks. The only crash-capable construct reachable is the deliberate `stack_smash()` in finding 1. No accidental panic found.\n* **Allocator model** (`Arena`, `translation/src/dag_lib.rs:114-296`): tcache LIFO/7, coalescing, top-chunk absorption, unsorted/binned ordering, and both glibc double-free diagnostics. 250 alloc/free-heavy sessions plus hand-built cases matched exactly, including `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` **and** `double free or corruption (!prev)` with identical 4096/8192-byte truncated stdout and SIGABRT.\n\n### 3. Known-unreproducible C behaviour (expected, not bugs)\n\n* The bytes a freed node's `city_name` prints as are a heap address (`PROTECT_PTR(NULL)`), so the C output differs on every run (e.g. `printf '1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n'`). The Rust emits a fixed placeholder of the same shape; everything around it matches. This accounted for every \"difference\" my un-normalised fuzzers reported.\n* Which signal the smashed stack raises (SIGSEGV ~80% / SIGBUS ~20% in the C) is nondeterministic; the Rust always raises SIGSEGV. Both documented in `translation/ERRORS.md` §3.","usage":{"total_tokens":175833,"tool_uses":48,"duration_ms":1115438},"uuid":"c4a17f95-db20-4057-b8cc-9d54462b92d6","session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752"}
