{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_igsly3vcf3nygd6o4y6qt7ervey6gex2j4qtrb4pdzumyakdzjya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01FPUWZHnbxrxuM3PzDg9Z6R","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","old_string":"/// A distance sum that overflows `int` can lower the distance of a node that\n/// was already visited, which rewrites its `previous` pointer and can make the\n/// `previous` chain cyclic.  `find_shortest_path` then writes past the end of\n/// its `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` stack array for ever: the C program dies from\n/// a signal without printing anything more.","new_string":"/// A chain of `k` cities plus one back-edge whose weight makes the distance sum\n/// overflow.  The back-edge sits on the second-to-last city, so it is explored\n/// before the loop reaches the end city, and it lowers the (already final)\n/// distance of city `j` - which rewrites `previous` and makes the chain cyclic.\nfn overflow_back_edge(k: usize, j: usize) -> String {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{}\\nC{j}\\n2147483647\\n\", k - 1));\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"5\\nC1\\nC{k}\\n8\\n\"));\n    input\n}\n\n/// Reconstructing such a cyclic chain runs past the end of\n/// `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]`, and gcc puts `path` directly below the `state`\n/// array: the writes land in `state`, which the loop itself reads back.  Most\n/// of the time that stops the loop - `state[0].node` becomes the current node,\n/// so the search finds index 0 and takes `state[0].previous`, which is NULL for\n/// the start node - and the C program happily prints a 101 entry path and exits\n/// 0.  It must not be mistaken for a crash.\n#[test]\nfn path_reconstruction_overruns_into_the_dijkstra_state() {\n    // the plain 101 entry case, spelled out\n    assert_identical(\n        \"101 entry path\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nB\\n2147483647\\n\\\n          2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // Back-edges into the start city keep the chain going for a few more words,\n    // which land in state[0].distance, state[0].previous, state[0].visited and\n    // then in state[1]... - 107, 111 and 110 entries respectively.\n    for (k, j) in [(4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 1), (8, 2), (10, 2)] {\n        assert_identical(\n            &format!(\"chain of {k} with a back-edge to C{j}\"),\n            overflow_back_edge(k, j).as_bytes(),\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n/// The same overrun, but for these graphs the writes keep going until they are\n/// past the `state` array as well and the frame is destroyed: the C program dies\n/// from a signal without printing anything more."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1171,"cache_read_input_tokens":308606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1171,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"af9fbfc0-2933-4bad-b9eb-0d577fb45296","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:20:32.841Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01FPUWZHnbxrxuM3PzDg9Z6R","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"93d1a345-f5aa-4ac0-9caf-d3183c552bf4","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:20:32.852Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","oldString":"/// A distance sum that overflows `int` can lower the distance of a node that\n/// was already visited, which rewrites its `previous` pointer and can make the\n/// `previous` chain cyclic.  `find_shortest_path` then writes past the end of\n/// its `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` stack array for ever: the C program dies from\n/// a signal without printing anything more.","newString":"/// A chain of `k` cities plus one back-edge whose weight makes the distance sum\n/// overflow.  The back-edge sits on the second-to-last city, so it is explored\n/// before the loop reaches the end city, and it lowers the (already final)\n/// distance of city `j` - which rewrites `previous` and makes the chain cyclic.\nfn overflow_back_edge(k: usize, j: usize) -> String {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{}\\nC{j}\\n2147483647\\n\", k - 1));\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"5\\nC1\\nC{k}\\n8\\n\"));\n    input\n}\n\n/// Reconstructing such a cyclic chain runs past the end of\n/// `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]`, and gcc puts `path` directly below the `state`\n/// array: the writes land in `state`, which the loop itself reads back.  Most\n/// of the time that stops the loop - `state[0].node` becomes the current node,\n/// so the search finds index 0 and takes `state[0].previous`, which is NULL for\n/// the start node - and the C program happily prints a 101 entry path and exits\n/// 0.  It must not be mistaken for a crash.\n#[test]\nfn path_reconstruction_overruns_into_the_dijkstra_state() {\n    // the plain 101 entry case, spelled out\n    assert_identical(\n        \"101 entry path\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nB\\n2147483647\\n\\\n          2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // Back-edges into the start city keep the chain going for a few more words,\n    // which land in state[0].distance, state[0].previous, state[0].visited and\n    // then in state[1]... - 107, 111 and 110 entries respectively.\n    for (k, j) in [(4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 1), (8, 2), (10, 2)] {\n        assert_identical(\n            &format!(\"chain of {k} with a back-edge to C{j}\"),\n            overflow_back_edge(k, j).as_bytes(),\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n/// The same overrun, but for these graphs the writes keep going until they are\n/// past the `state` array as well and the frame is destroyed: the C program dies\n/// from a signal without printing anything more.","originalFile":"//! Differential tests: run the original C program and the Rust translation as\n//! subprocesses, feed both the same bytes on stdin and require that stdout,\n//! stderr and the exit status are identical.\n//!\n//! Nothing here links against the translation as a library - the binary is\n//! driven exactly the way a shell drives it, because that is how the two\n//! programs are compared.\n\nuse std::ffi::OsStr;\nuse std::io::Write;\nuse std::os::unix::process::ExitStatusExt;\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\nuse std::process::{Command, Stdio};\nuse std::sync::{Mutex, OnceLock};\n\n/// The translation's binary; cargo builds it for us and hands over the path.\nconst RUST_BIN: &str = env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_driver\");\n\nfn repo_root() -> PathBuf {\n    // .../<root>/translation -> .../<root>\n    Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"))\n        .parent()\n        .expect(\"translation/ has a parent\")\n        .to_path_buf()\n}\n\n/// Path of the C `driver`, built with cmake on first use (Phase A build).\nfn c_bin() -> PathBuf {\n    static BUILT: OnceLock<Mutex<Option<PathBuf>>> = OnceLock::new();\n    let cell = BUILT.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(None));\n    let mut guard = cell.lock().unwrap();\n    if let Some(p) = guard.as_ref() {\n        return p.clone();\n    }\n\n    let c_src = repo_root().join(\"c_src\");\n    let build = c_src.join(\"build\");\n    let bin = build.join(\"driver\");\n\n    if !bin.exists() {\n        std::fs::create_dir_all(&build).expect(\"create c_src/build\");\n        run_tool(\"cmake\", &[OsStr::new(\"..\")], &build);\n        run_tool(\n            \"cmake\",\n            &[OsStr::new(\"--build\"), OsStr::new(\".\")],\n            &build,\n        );\n    }\n    assert!(\n        bin.exists(),\n        \"the C program was not built at {}\",\n        bin.display()\n    );\n\n    *guard = Some(bin.clone());\n    bin\n}\n\nfn run_tool(prog: &str, args: &[&OsStr], cwd: &Path) {\n    let out = Command::new(prog)\n        .args(args)\n        .current_dir(cwd)\n        .output()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not run {prog}: {e}\"));\n    assert!(\n        out.status.success(),\n        \"{prog} {args:?} failed:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout),\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr)\n    );\n}\n\nstruct Run {\n    stdout: Vec<u8>,\n    stderr: Vec<u8>,\n    /// `Some(n)` for a normal exit, `None` when killed by a signal.\n    code: Option<i32>,\n    /// `Some(n)` when killed by signal `n` (e.g. 6 = SIGABRT).\n    signal: Option<i32>,\n}\n\nfn run(bin: &Path, input: &[u8]) -> Run {\n    let mut child = Command::new(bin)\n        .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n        .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n        .stderr(Stdio::piped())\n        .spawn()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not spawn {}: {e}\", bin.display()));\n\n    // Feed stdin from another thread so a program that produces more output\n    // than a pipe holds can never dead-lock against us.\n    let mut stdin = child.stdin.take().expect(\"piped stdin\");\n    let bytes = input.to_vec();\n    let writer = std::thread::spawn(move || {\n        let _ = stdin.write_all(&bytes);\n        let _ = stdin.flush();\n        // dropping `stdin` closes it, which is the EOF the program waits for\n    });\n\n    let out = child.wait_with_output().expect(\"wait for child\");\n    writer.join().expect(\"stdin writer thread\");\n\n    Run {\n        stdout: out.stdout,\n        stderr: out.stderr,\n        code: out.status.code(),\n        signal: out.status.signal(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Readable rendering of program output for failure messages.\nfn show(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let mut s = String::new();\n    for &b in bytes {\n        match b {\n            b'\\n' => s.push_str(\"\\\\n\\n\"),\n            b'\\t' => s.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            0x20..=0x7e => s.push(b as char),\n            _ => s.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\x{b:02x}\")),\n        }\n    }\n    s\n}\n\n/// First index at which two byte strings differ, with a little context.\nfn first_diff(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let at = a\n        .iter()\n        .zip(b.iter())\n        .position(|(x, y)| x != y)\n        .unwrap_or(a.len().min(b.len()));\n    let from = at.saturating_sub(60);\n    format!(\n        \"first difference at byte {at} (C len {}, Rust len {})\\n\\\n         C    ...{}\\n\\\n         Rust ...{}\",\n        a.len(),\n        b.len(),\n        show(&a[from..(at + 60).min(a.len())]),\n        show(&b[from..(at + 60).min(b.len())]),\n    )\n}\n\n/// The whole point: identical stdout, identical stderr, identical exit status.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_identical(name: &str, input: &[u8]) {\n    let c = run(&c_bin(), input);\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n\n    assert!(\n        c.stdout == r.stdout,\n        \"[{name}] stdout differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.stderr == r.stderr,\n        \"[{name}] stderr differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.code == r.code && c.signal == r.signal,\n        \"[{name}] exit status differs: C code={:?} signal={:?}, Rust code={:?} signal={:?}\\ninput:\\n{}\",\n        c.code,\n        c.signal,\n        r.code,\n        r.signal,\n        show(input)\n    );\n}\n\n/// Run a whole table of cases and report every failure at once.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_all(cases: &[(&str, String)]) {\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n    for (name, input) in cases {\n        let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        if c.stdout != r.stdout {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stdout: {}\", first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)));\n        }\n        if c.stderr != r.stderr {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stderr: {}\", first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)));\n        }\n        if c.code != r.code || c.signal != r.signal {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"[{name}] status: C code={:?} sig={:?} vs Rust code={:?} sig={:?}\",\n                c.code, c.signal, r.code, r.signal\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} case(s) differ:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\nfn s(x: &str) -> String {\n    x.to_string()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the menu loop itself: EOF, unparsable choices, out-of-range choices\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn menu_loop_and_choice_parsing() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        // fgets() returns NULL straight away: menu once, then fall out of the\n        // loop and free the graph.\n        (\"empty input\", s(\"\")),\n        (\"only newline\", s(\"\\n\")),\n        (\"only spaces\", s(\"   \\n\")),\n        (\"exit immediately\", s(\"8\\n\")),\n        (\"exit without trailing newline\", s(\"8\")),\n        // sscanf(\"%d\") != 1 -> \"Invalid input\"\n        (\"letters\", s(\"abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"punctuation\", s(\"!!\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty line then exit\", s(\"\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"hex looks like 0\", s(\"0x8\\n8\\n\")),\n        // parsed, but not a menu entry -> \"Invalid choice\"\n        (\"zero\", s(\"0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"nine\", s(\"9\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative\", s(\"-1\\n8\\n\")),\n        // sscanf skips leading whitespace and accepts a sign / trailing junk\n        (\"leading blanks\", s(\"   3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading tab\", s(\"\\t8\\n\")),\n        (\"plus sign\", s(\"+3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"minus zero\", s(\"-0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"trailing junk\", s(\"3abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading zero\", s(\"08\\n\")),\n        // glibc: strtol saturates at LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN, the store truncates to int\n        (\"int overflow 2^31\", s(\"2147483648\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"wraps to 8\", s(\"4294967304\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long overflow -> -1\", s(\"99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative long overflow\", s(\"-99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        // no menu entry runs after the last line, the loop just ends\n        (\"choice then EOF\", s(\"3\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn long_input_lines_are_split_by_fgets() {\n    // fgets() stops after 255 bytes, so the tail of an over-long line is read\n    // as the *next* line - which the menu then tries to parse as a choice.\n    let long_name = \"x\".repeat(300);\n    let long_choice = format!(\"8{}\", \"0\".repeat(300));\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"300 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"255 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"y\".repeat(255))),\n        (\"256 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"z\".repeat(256))),\n        (\"254 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"w\".repeat(254))),\n        (\"300 byte choice line\", format!(\"{long_choice}\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long line then EOF\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn newline_and_nul_handling() {\n    // strcspn(input, \"\\n\") stops at the NUL as well, and a lone \\r stays part\n    // of the city name.\n    assert_identical(\"NUL inside the name\", b\"1\\nAB\\x00CD\\n3\\n4\\nAB\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"NUL first\", b\"1\\n\\x00AB\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"CRLF line endings\", b\"1\\r\\nA\\r\\n3\\r\\n8\\r\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"no trailing newline\", b\"1\\nA\\n3\\n8\");\n    assert_identical(\"blank city name\", b\"1\\n\\n3\\n4\\n\\n8\\n\");\n    // city names are raw bytes, not text: nothing may re-encode them\n    assert_identical(\n        \"invalid UTF-8 in the name\",\n        b\"1\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n3\\n4\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n5\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\\n          6\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n7\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\"high bytes only\", b\"1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    // leading/trailing blanks are part of the name, strcmp is exact\n    assert_identical(\n        \"blanks are part of the name\",\n        b\"1\\n  New York  \\n3\\n4\\n  New York  \\n4\\nNew York\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // a city called \"8\" must not be mistaken for a menu choice\n    assert_identical(\"city named 8\", b\"1\\n8\\n3\\n4\\n8\\n7\\n8\\n8\\n\");\n    // sscanf() and strcspn() both stop at the NUL, so the rest of the line is\n    // simply invisible\n    assert_identical(\"NUL inside the choice line\", b\"1\\x00abc\\nBoston\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"line starting with NUL\", b\"\\x008\\n8\\n\");\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 1: add city\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_city() {\n    let sixty_three = \"1234567890\".repeat(6) + \"123\"; // 63 bytes\n    let sixty_four = sixty_three.clone() + \"4\";\n    let seventy = \"a\".repeat(70);\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"one city\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"two cities\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate empty name\", s(\"1\\n\\n1\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"70 byte name is truncated\", format!(\"1\\n{seventy}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"63 byte name fits\", format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncated to the same 63 bytes, but strcmp compares against the full\n        // string, so the duplicate check does not fire\n        (\n            \"names equal after truncation\",\n            format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n1\\n{sixty_four}\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // EOF at the \"Enter city name:\" prompt\n        (\"EOF after choice 1\", s(\"1\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF after choice 1, no newline\", s(\"1\")),\n        (\"name without newline at EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn graph_is_full_at_max_nodes() {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=101 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"101 cities\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // add_node() tests \"graph is full\" *before* the duplicate check, so at\n    // capacity even a duplicate name is refused with \"Graph is full\".\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"1\\nN1\\n1\\nZZ\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"duplicate name at capacity\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 2: add route\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_route() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"both cities missing\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"to city missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"from city missing\", s(\"1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance zero\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"unparsable distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\nxyz\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty distance line\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance with junk\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5xyz\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-5\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncation makes these negative / positive again\n        (\"distance 2^31\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483648\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance 2^32+5\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n4294967301\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance long overflow\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"distance negative overflow\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"distance INT_MAX\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n2\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // main() parses the distance before it looks the cities up, so an\n        // unparsable distance wins over a missing city\n        (\"missing cities and a bad distance\", s(\"2\\nX\\nY\\nabc\\n8\\n\")),\n        // ... but a negative one is only caught inside add_edge, after the\n        // lookups, so there the missing city wins\n        (\"missing city and a negative distance\", s(\"2\\nX\\nY\\n-1\\n8\\n\")),\n        // EOF at each of the three prompts\n        (\"EOF at from prompt\", s(\"2\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at to prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at distance prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn node_has_maximum_edges() {\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nA\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nA\\nC{i}\\n{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"4\\nA\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"11 edges on one node\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // add_edge() checks the edge count first, the distance second and the\n    // duplicate last, so a full node reports \"maximum edges\" even for a\n    // negative distance, and a duplicate edge with a negative distance\n    // reports the distance.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nA\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nA\\nC{i}\\n{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nA\\nC11\\n-5\\n2\\nA\\nC1\\n-5\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"validation order in add_edge\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// cases 3 and 4: show all cities / show city details\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn show_cities_and_details() {\n    let mut big = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=40 {\n        big.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nCity{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    big.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"show empty graph\", s(\"3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"show twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n3\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of missing city\", s(\"4\\nNope\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city without edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city with edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n4\\nA\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at details prompt\", s(\"4\\n\")),\n        // more than one 4096 byte stdout block, so the write boundaries matter\n        (\"40 cities\", big),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 5: shortest path\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"start missing\", s(\"5\\nX\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"end missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"start is end\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"no edges at all\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"edge only backwards\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"two hops beat the direct edge\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n20\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"direct edge beats two hops\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"zero weight edges\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n0\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // an unreachable third city keeps its INT_MAX distance\n        (\n            \"unreachable city\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // INT_MAX is exactly the \"infinity\" the algorithm uses, so the\n        // neighbour never gets updated and no path is found\n        (\n            \"edge of length INT_MAX\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // 2147483646 + 2147483646 overflows int\n        (\n            \"distance sum overflows\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483646\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2147483646\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"cycle between two cities\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n5\\nB\\nA\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n5\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"EOF at start prompt\", s(\"5\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at end prompt\", s(\"5\\nA\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path_long_chain() {\n    // a 30 hop chain: exercises the path reconstruction and its malloc\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n{i}\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n5\\nC30\\nC1\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"30 city chain\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hundred_node_graph() {\n    // MAX_NODES cities in a chain: the Dijkstra state array and the\n    // reconstructed path both reach exactly MAX_NODES entries.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    let chain = input.clone() + \"5\\nC1\\nC100\\n6\\nC1\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city chain\", chain.as_bytes());\n\n    // ... and the same with an edge closing the cycle\n    let cyclic = input + \"2\\nC100\\nC1\\n1\\n5\\nC1\\nC100\\n5\\nC50\\nC49\\n6\\nC1\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city cycle\", cyclic.as_bytes());\n}\n\n/// A distance sum that overflows `int` can lower the distance of a node that\n/// was already visited, which rewrites its `previous` pointer and can make the\n/// `previous` chain cyclic.  `find_shortest_path` then writes past the end of\n/// its `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` stack array for ever: the C program dies from\n/// a signal without printing anything more.\n///\n/// Which signal it is (SIGSEGV or, less often, SIGBUS) depends on what the\n/// runaway writes clobber first and varies between runs of the *C* program, so\n/// the assertion is \"both die from a signal, with identical output\".\n#[test]\nfn distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic() {\n    let cycle = \"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2000000000\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2000000000\\n\\\n                 2\\nB\\nA\\n2000000000\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\";\n\n    for (name, input) in [\n        (\"crash with an empty stdout buffer\", cycle.to_string()),\n        // enough output beforehand that whole 4096 byte blocks have already\n        // been written when the process dies\n        (\"crash after one full stdout block\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=10 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n    ] {\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        for _ in 0..3 {\n            let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stdout),\n                show(&r.stdout),\n                \"[{name}] stdout must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stderr),\n                show(&r.stderr),\n                \"[{name}] stderr must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(c.code, None, \"[{name}] the C program must die by signal\");\n            assert_eq!(r.code, None, \"[{name}] the Rust program must die by signal\");\n            // 11 = SIGSEGV, 7 = SIGBUS\n            assert!(\n                matches!(c.signal, Some(11) | Some(7)),\n                \"[{name}] unexpected C signal {:?}\",\n                c.signal\n            );\n            assert_eq!(r.signal, Some(11), \"[{name}] Rust must die from SIGSEGV\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 6: shallow copy\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shallow_copy() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"6\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice on the same city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"chain of three\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // increment_refs_recursive() must not loop forever on a cycle\n        (\n            \"cycle\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond counts a shared node once\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"copy of a leaf\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n6\\nB\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"6\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 7: delete node - reference counting and the freed-chunk behaviour\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn delete_node_reference_counting() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"7\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"7\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 2 -> 1: nothing is freed, the node stays usable\n        (\"after a shallow copy\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice after two copies\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 1 -> 0: free(), and the graph keeps the dangling pointer\n        (\"delete then exit\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"delete then EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n\")),\n        // free() overwrites the start of city_name, so strcmp() no longer\n        // matches the name: every later lookup reports \"not found\"\n        (\"delete twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route into a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"path from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"shallow copy of a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        // the duplicate check no longer matches either, so the name can be\n        // added a second time - and malloc hands back the very same chunk\n        (\"re-add after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"re-add under a new name\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n3\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first() {\n    // The graph keeps the old pointers, so print_graph() shows which chunk each\n    // new node landed in: this pins down malloc's reuse order.\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"two freed, two added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"interleaved free and alloc\",\n            s(\"1\\nN1\\n1\\nN2\\n1\\nN3\\n7\\nN2\\n1\\nX\\n7\\nN1\\n7\\nN3\\n1\\nY\\n1\\nZ\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"free and re-add three times\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit() {\n    // More than 7 chunks of the same size are freed, so the tcache overflows\n    // and the rest come back in a different order.\n    for (name, order) in [\n        (\"ascending\", (1..=9).collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n        (\"descending\", (1..=9).rev().collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n    ] {\n        let mut input = String::new();\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in &order {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n        assert_identical(&format!(\"9 nodes freed {name}\"), input.as_bytes());\n    }\n\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"16 nodes freed and re-added\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n/// With a full tcache bin the freed chunks go to the unsorted bin, and the next\n/// `malloc` empties that bin into the tcache oldest first before popping it -\n/// so the *newest* of those chunks is handed out first, whatever their\n/// addresses are.  Both orders are checked, because \"newest first\" and \"lowest\n/// address first\" only differ here.\n#[test]\nfn overflowing_the_tcache_hands_chunks_back_newest_first() {\n    for (name, first, second) in [(\"high then low\", 11, 9), (\"low then high\", 9, 11)] {\n        let mut input = String::new();\n        for i in 1..=12 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in 1..=7 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\")); // fills the tcache bin\n        }\n        // both of these are isolated: their neighbours are still allocated\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{first}\\n7\\nN{second}\\n\"));\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n        assert_identical(&format!(\"isolated overflow chunks, {name}\"), input.as_bytes());\n    }\n}\n\n/// `free()` only writes its metadata into the chunk that actually ends up on a\n/// list, so some freed nodes keep a readable `city_name` and stay findable.\n#[test]\nfn some_freed_nodes_keep_their_name() {\n    // The highest chunk borders the top chunk and is absorbed into it, which\n    // rewrites only the chunk header - N10 is still found by name afterwards,\n    // while the tcache'd N1 is not.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN10\\n4\\nN10\\n4\\nN1\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"chunk absorbed into the top chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // N9 is merged into the run that starts at N8, so `fd`/`bk` land in N8 and\n    // N9 keeps its name.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN8\\n7\\nN9\\n4\\nN9\\n4\\nN8\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"chunk merged into a free run\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation() {\n    // find_shortest_path() mallocs `count * 8` bytes for the result, which for a\n    // 30 node path is the same malloc size class as node_t itself - so the path\n    // can take a chunk off the node free list and give it back.  Both the\n    // \"tcache already drained\" and the \"tcache still full\" case must leave the\n    // reuse order of the following add_city calls alone.\n    let mut chain = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    // nine freed chunks: seven in the tcache, two on the overflow list\n    let mut freed = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n\n    let mut drained = chain.clone() + &freed;\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        drained.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nE{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    drained.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n1\\nF1\\n1\\nF2\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a drained tcache\", drained.as_bytes());\n\n    let mut full = chain + &freed + \"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n\";\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        full.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nF{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    full.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a full tcache\", full.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints\n// a diagnostic and aborts, which kills the buffered stdout with it\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn double_free_aborts_the_process() {\n    // shallow_copy() walks the edges and revives the already freed B\n    // (ref_count 0 -> 1); free_graph() then frees it a second time.\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free of a tcache chunk\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free reached at EOF\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n\",\n    );\n\n    // The same, but the doubly freed chunk did not fit in the tcache, which\n    // glibc reports differently.  There is enough output here that the first\n    // 4096 byte block has already been written when the process dies.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nH\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nH\\nN9\\n5\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    // N9 is the highest chunk, so it is absorbed into the top chunk\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN9\\n6\\nH\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"double free of a chunk in the top chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // And once more with the doubly freed chunk on a bin instead (N10 stays\n    // allocated above it), which glibc reports differently again.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nH\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nH\\nN9\\n5\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN9\\n6\\nH\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"double free of a binned chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// longer sessions that mix everything\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn mixed_sessions() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\n            \"full walk through the menu\",\n            s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n1\\nAustin\\n2\\nBoston\\nDenver\\n100\\n\\\n               2\\nDenver\\nAustin\\n200\\n2\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n500\\n3\\n4\\nBoston\\n\\\n               5\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n6\\nBoston\\n3\\n7\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"errors everywhere\",\n            s(\"nope\\n0\\n99\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n4\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n\\\n               2\\nA\\nA\\n-1\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"no exit command, just EOF\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"menu after every operation\",\n            s(\"3\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n3\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the one input class whose C output is not reproducible: printing a node\n// whose chunk is on a free list.  city_name then holds glibc's free-list\n// metadata, i.e. a mangled heap pointer, so the C program prints different\n// bytes on every run.  This test proves that (rather than quietly skipping the\n// case) and still checks everything around it.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c() {\n    let input = b\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    let runs: Vec<Run> = (0..6).map(|_| run(&c_bin(), input)).collect();\n\n    // stderr and the exit status are stable, so they must match exactly.\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(c.stderr, r.stderr, \"stderr must match\");\n        assert_eq!((c.code, c.signal), (r.code, r.signal), \"status must match\");\n    }\n\n    // The C program disagrees with itself about the freed node's name, so no\n    // translation can match those bytes.  Everything up to the name does match.\n    let differ = runs.iter().any(|c| c.stdout != runs[0].stdout);\n    let aslr = std::fs::read_to_string(\"/proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space\")\n        .map(|s| s.trim() != \"0\")\n        .unwrap_or(true);\n    assert!(\n        differ || !aslr,\n        \"ASLR is on, yet the C program printed the freed node identically {} \\\n         times - if the heap layout really is reproducible then the Rust output \\\n         has to match those bytes, which it cannot, so this case needs \\\n         revisiting\",\n        runs.len()\n    );\n\n    let prefix = b\"City: \";\n    let at = runs[0]\n        .stdout\n        .windows(prefix.len())\n        .position(|w| w == prefix)\n        .expect(\"the freed node is printed\");\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(\n            &c.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            &r.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            \"output before the freed node's name must match\"\n        );\n    }\n    // ... and the ref_count printed after the garbled name is still the 0 that\n    // free() left behind, in both programs.\n    assert!(\n        r.stdout.ends_with(\n            b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"\n        ),\n        \"unexpected Rust tail:\\n{}\",\n        show(&r.stdout)\n    );\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert!(\n            c.stdout.ends_with(b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"),\n            \"unexpected C tail:\\n{}\",\n            show(&c.stdout)\n        );\n    }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":517,"oldLines":11,"newStart":517,"newLines":52,"lines":["     assert_identical(\"100 city cycle\", cyclic.as_bytes());"," }"," ","-/// A distance sum that overflows `int` can lower the distance of a node that","-/// was already visited, which rewrites its `previous` pointer and can make the","-/// `previous` chain cyclic.  `find_shortest_path` then writes past the end of","-/// its `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]` stack array for ever: the C program dies from","-/// a signal without printing anything more.","+/// A chain of `k` cities plus one back-edge whose weight makes the distance sum","+/// overflow.  The back-edge sits on the second-to-last city, so it is explored","+/// before the loop reaches the end city, and it lowers the (already final)","+/// distance of city `j` - which rewrites `previous` and makes the chain cyclic.","+fn overflow_back_edge(k: usize, j: usize) -> String {","+    let mut input = String::new();","+    for i in 1..=k {","+        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));","+    }","+    for i in 1..k {","+        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));","+    }","+    input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{}\\nC{j}\\n2147483647\\n\", k - 1));","+    input.push_str(&format!(\"5\\nC1\\nC{k}\\n8\\n\"));","+    input","+}","+","+/// Reconstructing such a cyclic chain runs past the end of","+/// `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]`, and gcc puts `path` directly below the `state`","+/// array: the writes land in `state`, which the loop itself reads back.  Most","+/// of the time that stops the loop - `state[0].node` becomes the current node,","+/// so the search finds index 0 and takes `state[0].previous`, which is NULL for","+/// the start node - and the C program happily prints a 101 entry path and exits","+/// 0.  It must not be mistaken for a crash.","+#[test]","+fn path_reconstruction_overruns_into_the_dijkstra_state() {","+    // the plain 101 entry case, spelled out","+    assert_identical(","+        \"101 entry path\",","+        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nB\\n2147483647\\n\\","+          2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\",","+    );","+    // Back-edges into the start city keep the chain going for a few more words,","+    // which land in state[0].distance, state[0].previous, state[0].visited and","+    // then in state[1]... - 107, 111 and 110 entries respectively.","+    for (k, j) in [(4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 1), (8, 2), (10, 2)] {","+        assert_identical(","+            &format!(\"chain of {k} with a back-edge to C{j}\"),","+            overflow_back_edge(k, j).as_bytes(),","+        );","+    }","+}","+","+/// The same overrun, but for these graphs the writes keep going until they are","+/// past the `state` array as well and the frame is destroyed: the C program dies","+/// from a signal without printing anything more."," ///"," /// Which signal it is (SIGSEGV or, less often, SIGBUS) depends on what the"," /// runaway writes clobber first and varies between runs of the *C* program, so"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_24pbwjk3mo2wti7qayyqfkdpk5eh2elewfse6r5ewagpypt3neia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01VHFq84az3SF5AWn9XWR4cy","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","old_string":"        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n    ] {","new_string":"        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n        // chains whose overrun does not settle down inside `state` either\n        (\"3 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(3, 1)),\n        (\"4 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(4, 1)),\n        (\"8 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(8, 1)),\n        (\"10 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(10, 1)),\n    ] {"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1620,"cache_read_input_tokens":309777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1620,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"7543b105-52e4-4e82-86d7-060e215c891e","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:20:40.073Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01VHFq84az3SF5AWn9XWR4cy","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"082de683-dcf5-47c5-8cf1-7947dd50051e","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:20:40.085Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/tests/differential.rs","oldString":"        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n    ] {","newString":"        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n        // chains whose overrun does not settle down inside `state` either\n        (\"3 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(3, 1)),\n        (\"4 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(4, 1)),\n        (\"8 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(8, 1)),\n        (\"10 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(10, 1)),\n    ] {","originalFile":"//! Differential tests: run the original C program and the Rust translation as\n//! subprocesses, feed both the same bytes on stdin and require that stdout,\n//! stderr and the exit status are identical.\n//!\n//! Nothing here links against the translation as a library - the binary is\n//! driven exactly the way a shell drives it, because that is how the two\n//! programs are compared.\n\nuse std::ffi::OsStr;\nuse std::io::Write;\nuse std::os::unix::process::ExitStatusExt;\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\nuse std::process::{Command, Stdio};\nuse std::sync::{Mutex, OnceLock};\n\n/// The translation's binary; cargo builds it for us and hands over the path.\nconst RUST_BIN: &str = env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_driver\");\n\nfn repo_root() -> PathBuf {\n    // .../<root>/translation -> .../<root>\n    Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"))\n        .parent()\n        .expect(\"translation/ has a parent\")\n        .to_path_buf()\n}\n\n/// Path of the C `driver`, built with cmake on first use (Phase A build).\nfn c_bin() -> PathBuf {\n    static BUILT: OnceLock<Mutex<Option<PathBuf>>> = OnceLock::new();\n    let cell = BUILT.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(None));\n    let mut guard = cell.lock().unwrap();\n    if let Some(p) = guard.as_ref() {\n        return p.clone();\n    }\n\n    let c_src = repo_root().join(\"c_src\");\n    let build = c_src.join(\"build\");\n    let bin = build.join(\"driver\");\n\n    if !bin.exists() {\n        std::fs::create_dir_all(&build).expect(\"create c_src/build\");\n        run_tool(\"cmake\", &[OsStr::new(\"..\")], &build);\n        run_tool(\n            \"cmake\",\n            &[OsStr::new(\"--build\"), OsStr::new(\".\")],\n            &build,\n        );\n    }\n    assert!(\n        bin.exists(),\n        \"the C program was not built at {}\",\n        bin.display()\n    );\n\n    *guard = Some(bin.clone());\n    bin\n}\n\nfn run_tool(prog: &str, args: &[&OsStr], cwd: &Path) {\n    let out = Command::new(prog)\n        .args(args)\n        .current_dir(cwd)\n        .output()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not run {prog}: {e}\"));\n    assert!(\n        out.status.success(),\n        \"{prog} {args:?} failed:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout),\n        String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr)\n    );\n}\n\nstruct Run {\n    stdout: Vec<u8>,\n    stderr: Vec<u8>,\n    /// `Some(n)` for a normal exit, `None` when killed by a signal.\n    code: Option<i32>,\n    /// `Some(n)` when killed by signal `n` (e.g. 6 = SIGABRT).\n    signal: Option<i32>,\n}\n\nfn run(bin: &Path, input: &[u8]) -> Run {\n    let mut child = Command::new(bin)\n        .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n        .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n        .stderr(Stdio::piped())\n        .spawn()\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"could not spawn {}: {e}\", bin.display()));\n\n    // Feed stdin from another thread so a program that produces more output\n    // than a pipe holds can never dead-lock against us.\n    let mut stdin = child.stdin.take().expect(\"piped stdin\");\n    let bytes = input.to_vec();\n    let writer = std::thread::spawn(move || {\n        let _ = stdin.write_all(&bytes);\n        let _ = stdin.flush();\n        // dropping `stdin` closes it, which is the EOF the program waits for\n    });\n\n    let out = child.wait_with_output().expect(\"wait for child\");\n    writer.join().expect(\"stdin writer thread\");\n\n    Run {\n        stdout: out.stdout,\n        stderr: out.stderr,\n        code: out.status.code(),\n        signal: out.status.signal(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Readable rendering of program output for failure messages.\nfn show(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let mut s = String::new();\n    for &b in bytes {\n        match b {\n            b'\\n' => s.push_str(\"\\\\n\\n\"),\n            b'\\t' => s.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            0x20..=0x7e => s.push(b as char),\n            _ => s.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\x{b:02x}\")),\n        }\n    }\n    s\n}\n\n/// First index at which two byte strings differ, with a little context.\nfn first_diff(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> String {\n    let at = a\n        .iter()\n        .zip(b.iter())\n        .position(|(x, y)| x != y)\n        .unwrap_or(a.len().min(b.len()));\n    let from = at.saturating_sub(60);\n    format!(\n        \"first difference at byte {at} (C len {}, Rust len {})\\n\\\n         C    ...{}\\n\\\n         Rust ...{}\",\n        a.len(),\n        b.len(),\n        show(&a[from..(at + 60).min(a.len())]),\n        show(&b[from..(at + 60).min(b.len())]),\n    )\n}\n\n/// The whole point: identical stdout, identical stderr, identical exit status.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_identical(name: &str, input: &[u8]) {\n    let c = run(&c_bin(), input);\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n\n    assert!(\n        c.stdout == r.stdout,\n        \"[{name}] stdout differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.stderr == r.stderr,\n        \"[{name}] stderr differs\\ninput:\\n{}\\n{}\",\n        show(input),\n        first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)\n    );\n    assert!(\n        c.code == r.code && c.signal == r.signal,\n        \"[{name}] exit status differs: C code={:?} signal={:?}, Rust code={:?} signal={:?}\\ninput:\\n{}\",\n        c.code,\n        c.signal,\n        r.code,\n        r.signal,\n        show(input)\n    );\n}\n\n/// Run a whole table of cases and report every failure at once.\n#[track_caller]\nfn assert_all(cases: &[(&str, String)]) {\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n    for (name, input) in cases {\n        let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        if c.stdout != r.stdout {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stdout: {}\", first_diff(&c.stdout, &r.stdout)));\n        }\n        if c.stderr != r.stderr {\n            failures.push(format!(\"[{name}] stderr: {}\", first_diff(&c.stderr, &r.stderr)));\n        }\n        if c.code != r.code || c.signal != r.signal {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"[{name}] status: C code={:?} sig={:?} vs Rust code={:?} sig={:?}\",\n                c.code, c.signal, r.code, r.signal\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} case(s) differ:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\nfn s(x: &str) -> String {\n    x.to_string()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the menu loop itself: EOF, unparsable choices, out-of-range choices\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn menu_loop_and_choice_parsing() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        // fgets() returns NULL straight away: menu once, then fall out of the\n        // loop and free the graph.\n        (\"empty input\", s(\"\")),\n        (\"only newline\", s(\"\\n\")),\n        (\"only spaces\", s(\"   \\n\")),\n        (\"exit immediately\", s(\"8\\n\")),\n        (\"exit without trailing newline\", s(\"8\")),\n        // sscanf(\"%d\") != 1 -> \"Invalid input\"\n        (\"letters\", s(\"abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"punctuation\", s(\"!!\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty line then exit\", s(\"\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"hex looks like 0\", s(\"0x8\\n8\\n\")),\n        // parsed, but not a menu entry -> \"Invalid choice\"\n        (\"zero\", s(\"0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"nine\", s(\"9\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative\", s(\"-1\\n8\\n\")),\n        // sscanf skips leading whitespace and accepts a sign / trailing junk\n        (\"leading blanks\", s(\"   3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading tab\", s(\"\\t8\\n\")),\n        (\"plus sign\", s(\"+3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"minus zero\", s(\"-0\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"trailing junk\", s(\"3abc\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"leading zero\", s(\"08\\n\")),\n        // glibc: strtol saturates at LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN, the store truncates to int\n        (\"int overflow 2^31\", s(\"2147483648\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"wraps to 8\", s(\"4294967304\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long overflow -> -1\", s(\"99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative long overflow\", s(\"-99999999999999999999\\n8\\n\")),\n        // no menu entry runs after the last line, the loop just ends\n        (\"choice then EOF\", s(\"3\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn long_input_lines_are_split_by_fgets() {\n    // fgets() stops after 255 bytes, so the tail of an over-long line is read\n    // as the *next* line - which the menu then tries to parse as a choice.\n    let long_name = \"x\".repeat(300);\n    let long_choice = format!(\"8{}\", \"0\".repeat(300));\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"300 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"255 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"y\".repeat(255))),\n        (\"256 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"z\".repeat(256))),\n        (\"254 byte city name\", format!(\"1\\n{}\\n3\\n8\\n\", \"w\".repeat(254))),\n        (\"300 byte choice line\", format!(\"{long_choice}\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"long line then EOF\", format!(\"1\\n{long_name}\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn newline_and_nul_handling() {\n    // strcspn(input, \"\\n\") stops at the NUL as well, and a lone \\r stays part\n    // of the city name.\n    assert_identical(\"NUL inside the name\", b\"1\\nAB\\x00CD\\n3\\n4\\nAB\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"NUL first\", b\"1\\n\\x00AB\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"CRLF line endings\", b\"1\\r\\nA\\r\\n3\\r\\n8\\r\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"no trailing newline\", b\"1\\nA\\n3\\n8\");\n    assert_identical(\"blank city name\", b\"1\\n\\n3\\n4\\n\\n8\\n\");\n    // city names are raw bytes, not text: nothing may re-encode them\n    assert_identical(\n        \"invalid UTF-8 in the name\",\n        b\"1\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n3\\n4\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n5\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n\\\n          6\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n7\\n\\xff\\xfe\\xc3(\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\"high bytes only\", b\"1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n1\\n\\x80\\x81\\x82\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    // leading/trailing blanks are part of the name, strcmp is exact\n    assert_identical(\n        \"blanks are part of the name\",\n        b\"1\\n  New York  \\n3\\n4\\n  New York  \\n4\\nNew York\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // a city called \"8\" must not be mistaken for a menu choice\n    assert_identical(\"city named 8\", b\"1\\n8\\n3\\n4\\n8\\n7\\n8\\n8\\n\");\n    // sscanf() and strcspn() both stop at the NUL, so the rest of the line is\n    // simply invisible\n    assert_identical(\"NUL inside the choice line\", b\"1\\x00abc\\nBoston\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"line starting with NUL\", b\"\\x008\\n8\\n\");\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 1: add city\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_city() {\n    let sixty_three = \"1234567890\".repeat(6) + \"123\"; // 63 bytes\n    let sixty_four = sixty_three.clone() + \"4\";\n    let seventy = \"a\".repeat(70);\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"one city\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"two cities\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate empty name\", s(\"1\\n\\n1\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"70 byte name is truncated\", format!(\"1\\n{seventy}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"63 byte name fits\", format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncated to the same 63 bytes, but strcmp compares against the full\n        // string, so the duplicate check does not fire\n        (\n            \"names equal after truncation\",\n            format!(\"1\\n{sixty_three}\\n1\\n{sixty_four}\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // EOF at the \"Enter city name:\" prompt\n        (\"EOF after choice 1\", s(\"1\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF after choice 1, no newline\", s(\"1\")),\n        (\"name without newline at EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn graph_is_full_at_max_nodes() {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=101 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"101 cities\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // add_node() tests \"graph is full\" *before* the duplicate check, so at\n    // capacity even a duplicate name is refused with \"Graph is full\".\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"1\\nN1\\n1\\nZZ\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"duplicate name at capacity\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 2: add route\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn add_route() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"both cities missing\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"to city missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"from city missing\", s(\"1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance zero\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"unparsable distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\nxyz\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"empty distance line\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance with junk\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5xyz\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"negative distance\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-5\\n8\\n\")),\n        // truncation makes these negative / positive again\n        (\"distance 2^31\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483648\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance 2^32+5\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n4294967301\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"distance long overflow\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"distance negative overflow\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n-99999999999999999999\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"distance INT_MAX\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"duplicate self edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n2\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // main() parses the distance before it looks the cities up, so an\n        // unparsable distance wins over a missing city\n        (\"missing cities and a bad distance\", s(\"2\\nX\\nY\\nabc\\n8\\n\")),\n        // ... but a negative one is only caught inside add_edge, after the\n        // lookups, so there the missing city wins\n        (\"missing city and a negative distance\", s(\"2\\nX\\nY\\n-1\\n8\\n\")),\n        // EOF at each of the three prompts\n        (\"EOF at from prompt\", s(\"2\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at to prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at distance prompt\", s(\"2\\nA\\nB\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn node_has_maximum_edges() {\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nA\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nA\\nC{i}\\n{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"4\\nA\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"11 edges on one node\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // add_edge() checks the edge count first, the distance second and the\n    // duplicate last, so a full node reports \"maximum edges\" even for a\n    // negative distance, and a duplicate edge with a negative distance\n    // reports the distance.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nA\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=11 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nA\\nC{i}\\n{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nA\\nC11\\n-5\\n2\\nA\\nC1\\n-5\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"validation order in add_edge\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// cases 3 and 4: show all cities / show city details\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn show_cities_and_details() {\n    let mut big = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=40 {\n        big.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nCity{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    big.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"show empty graph\", s(\"3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"show twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n3\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of missing city\", s(\"4\\nNope\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city without edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details of city with edges\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n4\\nA\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at details prompt\", s(\"4\\n\")),\n        // more than one 4096 byte stdout block, so the write boundaries matter\n        (\"40 cities\", big),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 5: shortest path\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"start missing\", s(\"5\\nX\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"end missing\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nY\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"start is end\", s(\"1\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"no edges at all\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"edge only backwards\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single edge\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"two hops beat the direct edge\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n20\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"direct edge beats two hops\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n3\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"zero weight edges\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n0\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n0\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // an unreachable third city keeps its INT_MAX distance\n        (\n            \"unreachable city\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // INT_MAX is exactly the \"infinity\" the algorithm uses, so the\n        // neighbour never gets updated and no path is found\n        (\n            \"edge of length INT_MAX\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483647\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // 2147483646 + 2147483646 overflows int\n        (\n            \"distance sum overflows\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2147483646\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2147483646\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"cycle between two cities\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n5\\nB\\nA\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n5\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n2\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"EOF at start prompt\", s(\"5\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at end prompt\", s(\"5\\nA\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn shortest_path_long_chain() {\n    // a 30 hop chain: exercises the path reconstruction and its malloc\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n{i}\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n5\\nC30\\nC1\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"30 city chain\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hundred_node_graph() {\n    // MAX_NODES cities in a chain: the Dijkstra state array and the\n    // reconstructed path both reach exactly MAX_NODES entries.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..100 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    let chain = input.clone() + \"5\\nC1\\nC100\\n6\\nC1\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city chain\", chain.as_bytes());\n\n    // ... and the same with an edge closing the cycle\n    let cyclic = input + \"2\\nC100\\nC1\\n1\\n5\\nC1\\nC100\\n5\\nC50\\nC49\\n6\\nC1\\n8\\n\";\n    assert_identical(\"100 city cycle\", cyclic.as_bytes());\n}\n\n/// A chain of `k` cities plus one back-edge whose weight makes the distance sum\n/// overflow.  The back-edge sits on the second-to-last city, so it is explored\n/// before the loop reaches the end city, and it lowers the (already final)\n/// distance of city `j` - which rewrites `previous` and makes the chain cyclic.\nfn overflow_back_edge(k: usize, j: usize) -> String {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..k {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{}\\nC{j}\\n2147483647\\n\", k - 1));\n    input.push_str(&format!(\"5\\nC1\\nC{k}\\n8\\n\"));\n    input\n}\n\n/// Reconstructing such a cyclic chain runs past the end of\n/// `node_t *path[MAX_NODES]`, and gcc puts `path` directly below the `state`\n/// array: the writes land in `state`, which the loop itself reads back.  Most\n/// of the time that stops the loop - `state[0].node` becomes the current node,\n/// so the search finds index 0 and takes `state[0].previous`, which is NULL for\n/// the start node - and the C program happily prints a 101 entry path and exits\n/// 0.  It must not be mistaken for a crash.\n#[test]\nfn path_reconstruction_overruns_into_the_dijkstra_state() {\n    // the plain 101 entry case, spelled out\n    assert_identical(\n        \"101 entry path\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nB\\n2147483647\\n\\\n          2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n5\\nA\\nD\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    // Back-edges into the start city keep the chain going for a few more words,\n    // which land in state[0].distance, state[0].previous, state[0].visited and\n    // then in state[1]... - 107, 111 and 110 entries respectively.\n    for (k, j) in [(4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 1), (8, 2), (10, 2)] {\n        assert_identical(\n            &format!(\"chain of {k} with a back-edge to C{j}\"),\n            overflow_back_edge(k, j).as_bytes(),\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n/// The same overrun, but for these graphs the writes keep going until they are\n/// past the `state` array as well and the frame is destroyed: the C program dies\n/// from a signal without printing anything more.\n///\n/// Which signal it is (SIGSEGV or, less often, SIGBUS) depends on what the\n/// runaway writes clobber first and varies between runs of the *C* program, so\n/// the assertion is \"both die from a signal, with identical output\".\n#[test]\nfn distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic() {\n    let cycle = \"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n2000000000\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n2000000000\\n\\\n                 2\\nB\\nA\\n2000000000\\n5\\nA\\nC\\n8\\n\";\n\n    for (name, input) in [\n        (\"crash with an empty stdout buffer\", cycle.to_string()),\n        // enough output beforehand that whole 4096 byte blocks have already\n        // been written when the process dies\n        (\"crash after one full stdout block\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=10 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n        (\"crash after two full stdout blocks\", {\n            let mut s = String::new();\n            for i in 1..=40 {\n                s.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nP{i}\\n\"));\n            }\n            s + cycle\n        }),\n    ] {\n        let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());\n        for _ in 0..3 {\n            let c = run(&c_bin(), input.as_bytes());\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stdout),\n                show(&r.stdout),\n                \"[{name}] stdout must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(\n                show(&c.stderr),\n                show(&r.stderr),\n                \"[{name}] stderr must match\"\n            );\n            assert_eq!(c.code, None, \"[{name}] the C program must die by signal\");\n            assert_eq!(r.code, None, \"[{name}] the Rust program must die by signal\");\n            // 11 = SIGSEGV, 7 = SIGBUS\n            assert!(\n                matches!(c.signal, Some(11) | Some(7)),\n                \"[{name}] unexpected C signal {:?}\",\n                c.signal\n            );\n            assert_eq!(r.signal, Some(11), \"[{name}] Rust must die from SIGSEGV\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 6: shallow copy\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn shallow_copy() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"6\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"single city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice on the same city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"chain of three\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nC\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        // increment_refs_recursive() must not loop forever on a cycle\n        (\n            \"cycle\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"self loop\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nA\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"diamond counts a shared node once\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n2\\nA\\nC\\n1\\n2\\nB\\nD\\n1\\n2\\nC\\nD\\n1\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"copy of a leaf\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n6\\nB\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"6\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// case 7: delete node - reference counting and the freed-chunk behaviour\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn delete_node_reference_counting() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"city missing\", s(\"7\\nZ\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"EOF at prompt\", s(\"7\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 2 -> 1: nothing is freed, the node stays usable\n        (\"after a shallow copy\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"twice after two copies\", s(\"1\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        // ref_count 1 -> 0: free(), and the graph keeps the dangling pointer\n        (\"delete then exit\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"delete then EOF\", s(\"1\\nBoston\\n7\\nBoston\\n\")),\n        // free() overwrites the start of city_name, so strcmp() no longer\n        // matches the name: every later lookup reports \"not found\"\n        (\"delete twice\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"details after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n4\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"route into a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"path from a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"shallow copy of a deleted city\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\")),\n        // the duplicate check no longer matches either, so the name can be\n        // added a second time - and malloc hands back the very same chunk\n        (\"re-add after delete\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\"re-add under a new name\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n3\\n4\\nB\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_are_reused_most_recent_first() {\n    // The graph keeps the old pointers, so print_graph() shows which chunk each\n    // new node landed in: this pins down malloc's reuse order.\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\"two freed, two added\", s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n7\\nA\\n7\\nB\\n1\\nC\\n1\\nD\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n        (\n            \"interleaved free and alloc\",\n            s(\"1\\nN1\\n1\\nN2\\n1\\nN3\\n7\\nN2\\n1\\nX\\n7\\nN1\\n7\\nN3\\n1\\nY\\n1\\nZ\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\"free and re-add three times\", s(\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\")),\n    ]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_beyond_the_tcache_limit() {\n    // More than 7 chunks of the same size are freed, so the tcache overflows\n    // and the rest come back in a different order.\n    for (name, order) in [\n        (\"ascending\", (1..=9).collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n        (\"descending\", (1..=9).rev().collect::<Vec<_>>()),\n    ] {\n        let mut input = String::new();\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in &order {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n        assert_identical(&format!(\"9 nodes freed {name}\"), input.as_bytes());\n    }\n\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=16 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"16 nodes freed and re-added\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n/// With a full tcache bin the freed chunks go to the unsorted bin, and the next\n/// `malloc` empties that bin into the tcache oldest first before popping it -\n/// so the *newest* of those chunks is handed out first, whatever their\n/// addresses are.  Both orders are checked, because \"newest first\" and \"lowest\n/// address first\" only differ here.\n#[test]\nfn overflowing_the_tcache_hands_chunks_back_newest_first() {\n    for (name, first, second) in [(\"high then low\", 11, 9), (\"low then high\", 9, 11)] {\n        let mut input = String::new();\n        for i in 1..=12 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        for i in 1..=7 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\")); // fills the tcache bin\n        }\n        // both of these are isolated: their neighbours are still allocated\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{first}\\n7\\nN{second}\\n\"));\n        for i in 1..=9 {\n            input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nM{i}\\n\"));\n        }\n        input.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n        assert_identical(&format!(\"isolated overflow chunks, {name}\"), input.as_bytes());\n    }\n}\n\n/// `free()` only writes its metadata into the chunk that actually ends up on a\n/// list, so some freed nodes keep a readable `city_name` and stay findable.\n#[test]\nfn some_freed_nodes_keep_their_name() {\n    // The highest chunk borders the top chunk and is absorbed into it, which\n    // rewrites only the chunk header - N10 is still found by name afterwards,\n    // while the tcache'd N1 is not.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN10\\n4\\nN10\\n4\\nN1\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"chunk absorbed into the top chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // N9 is merged into the run that starts at N8, so `fd`/`bk` land in N8 and\n    // N9 keeps its name.\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN8\\n7\\nN9\\n4\\nN9\\n4\\nN8\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"chunk merged into a free run\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn freed_chunks_and_the_shortest_path_allocation() {\n    // find_shortest_path() mallocs `count * 8` bytes for the result, which for a\n    // 30 node path is the same malloc size class as node_t itself - so the path\n    // can take a chunk off the node free list and give it back.  Both the\n    // \"tcache already drained\" and the \"tcache still full\" case must leave the\n    // reuse order of the following add_city calls alone.\n    let mut chain = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nC{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..30 {\n        chain.push_str(&format!(\"2\\nC{i}\\nC{}\\n1\\n\", i + 1));\n    }\n    // nine freed chunks: seven in the tcache, two on the overflow list\n    let mut freed = String::new();\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        freed.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nD{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n\n    let mut drained = chain.clone() + &freed;\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        drained.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nE{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    drained.push_str(\"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n1\\nF1\\n1\\nF2\\n3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a drained tcache\", drained.as_bytes());\n\n    let mut full = chain + &freed + \"5\\nC1\\nC30\\n\";\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        full.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nF{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    full.push_str(\"3\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"path allocation with a full tcache\", full.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the two ways this program manages to free the same chunk twice: glibc prints\n// a diagnostic and aborts, which kills the buffered stdout with it\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn double_free_aborts_the_process() {\n    // shallow_copy() walks the edges and revives the already freed B\n    // (ref_count 0 -> 1); free_graph() then frees it a second time.\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free of a tcache chunk\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n8\\n\",\n    );\n    assert_identical(\n        \"double free reached at EOF\",\n        b\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n5\\n7\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n\",\n    );\n\n    // The same, but the doubly freed chunk did not fit in the tcache, which\n    // glibc reports differently.  There is enough output here that the first\n    // 4096 byte block has already been written when the process dies.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nH\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=9 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nH\\nN9\\n5\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    // N9 is the highest chunk, so it is absorbed into the top chunk\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN9\\n6\\nH\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"double free of a chunk in the top chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n\n    // And once more with the doubly freed chunk on a bin instead (N10 stays\n    // allocated above it), which glibc reports differently again.\n    let mut input = String::from(\"1\\nH\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=10 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"1\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"2\\nH\\nN9\\n5\\n\");\n    for i in 1..=7 {\n        input.push_str(&format!(\"7\\nN{i}\\n\"));\n    }\n    input.push_str(\"7\\nN9\\n6\\nH\\n8\\n\");\n    assert_identical(\"double free of a binned chunk\", input.as_bytes());\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// longer sessions that mix everything\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn mixed_sessions() {\n    assert_all(&[\n        (\n            \"full walk through the menu\",\n            s(\"1\\nBoston\\n1\\nDenver\\n1\\nAustin\\n2\\nBoston\\nDenver\\n100\\n\\\n               2\\nDenver\\nAustin\\n200\\n2\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n500\\n3\\n4\\nBoston\\n\\\n               5\\nBoston\\nAustin\\n6\\nBoston\\n3\\n7\\nDenver\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"errors everywhere\",\n            s(\"nope\\n0\\n99\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n1\\n4\\nA\\n5\\nA\\nB\\n6\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n1\\nA\\n\\\n               2\\nA\\nA\\n-1\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"no exit command, just EOF\",\n            s(\"1\\nA\\n1\\nB\\n2\\nA\\nB\\n7\\n3\\n\"),\n        ),\n        (\n            \"menu after every operation\",\n            s(\"3\\n1\\nA\\n3\\n4\\nA\\n3\\n6\\nA\\n3\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\"),\n        ),\n    ]);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the one input class whose C output is not reproducible: printing a node\n// whose chunk is on a free list.  city_name then holds glibc's free-list\n// metadata, i.e. a mangled heap pointer, so the C program prints different\n// bytes on every run.  This test proves that (rather than quietly skipping the\n// case) and still checks everything around it.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#[test]\nfn printing_a_freed_node_is_not_reproducible_in_c() {\n    let input = b\"1\\nA\\n7\\nA\\n3\\n8\\n\";\n    let runs: Vec<Run> = (0..6).map(|_| run(&c_bin(), input)).collect();\n\n    // stderr and the exit status are stable, so they must match exactly.\n    let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input);\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(c.stderr, r.stderr, \"stderr must match\");\n        assert_eq!((c.code, c.signal), (r.code, r.signal), \"status must match\");\n    }\n\n    // The C program disagrees with itself about the freed node's name, so no\n    // translation can match those bytes.  Everything up to the name does match.\n    let differ = runs.iter().any(|c| c.stdout != runs[0].stdout);\n    let aslr = std::fs::read_to_string(\"/proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space\")\n        .map(|s| s.trim() != \"0\")\n        .unwrap_or(true);\n    assert!(\n        differ || !aslr,\n        \"ASLR is on, yet the C program printed the freed node identically {} \\\n         times - if the heap layout really is reproducible then the Rust output \\\n         has to match those bytes, which it cannot, so this case needs \\\n         revisiting\",\n        runs.len()\n    );\n\n    let prefix = b\"City: \";\n    let at = runs[0]\n        .stdout\n        .windows(prefix.len())\n        .position(|w| w == prefix)\n        .expect(\"the freed node is printed\");\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert_eq!(\n            &c.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            &r.stdout[..at + prefix.len()],\n            \"output before the freed node's name must match\"\n        );\n    }\n    // ... and the ref_count printed after the garbled name is still the 0 that\n    // free() left behind, in both programs.\n    assert!(\n        r.stdout.ends_with(\n            b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"\n        ),\n        \"unexpected Rust tail:\\n{}\",\n        show(&r.stdout)\n    );\n    for c in &runs {\n        assert!(\n            c.stdout.ends_with(b\" (ref_count: 0)\\n  Edges:\\n\\n=== DAG City Route Manager ===\\n\\\n1. Add city (node)\\n2. Add route (edge)\\n3. Show all cities\\n4. Show city details\\n\\\n5. Find shortest path\\n6. Make shallow copy of subsection\\n7. Delete node\\n8. Exit\\n\\\nChoice: Freeing graph and exiting...\\n\"),\n            \"unexpected C tail:\\n{}\",\n            show(&c.stdout)\n        );\n    }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":590,"oldLines":6,"newStart":590,"newLines":11,"lines":["             }","             s + cycle","         }),","+        // chains whose overrun does not settle down inside `state` either","+        (\"3 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(3, 1)),","+        (\"4 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(4, 1)),","+        (\"8 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(8, 1)),","+        (\"10 city chain, back-edge to the start\", overflow_back_edge(10, 1)),","     ] {","         let r = run(Path::new(RUST_BIN), input.as_bytes());","         for _ in 0..3 {"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
