{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mrpzkk73bqpbxzxg5f6kv3duthdz4rz3uwf3r3iddvrjt3urkc3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01UeejDNnjYJK6tTCSFVUuAR","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-FbQLo9/translation/ERRORS.md","old_string":"### 3.2 Which signal the smashed stack produces (see 1.4)\n\nstdout, stderr and \"died from a signal\" are stable, but the C program dies from\nSIGSEGV in roughly four runs out of five and from SIGBUS otherwise, depending on\nwhat the runaway writes clobber first. The Rust program always raises SIGSEGV,\nthe common case; `distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic` accepts\neither signal from the C side and requires identical output.","new_string":"### 3.2 Which signal the smashed stack produces (see 1.4/1.5)\n\nFor the inputs where the overrun really does destroy the frame, stdout, stderr\nand \"died from a signal\" are stable, but the C program dies from SIGSEGV in\nroughly four runs out of five and from SIGBUS otherwise, depending on what the\nrunaway writes clobber first. The Rust program always raises SIGSEGV, the common\ncase; `distance_overflow_makes_the_previous_chain_cyclic` accepts either signal\nfrom the C side and requires identical output.\n\nNote that this is *only* about the inputs of 1.4. The much more common overrun of\n1.5 ends with a normal exit 0 and a fully deterministic 101-entry path, and that\nis compared byte for byte."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2408,"cache_read_input_tokens":318131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2408,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"869bac86-43a9-48c4-a3dc-356dcefd37aa","timestamp":"