{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zrnizwwhnqgvrr37s6caokxesxsftu325pspxc5nrfkcfkyjelrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and verified. Final state:\n\n## Phase A — both build and run\n- C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver` (compiled clean, no warnings)\n\n## Phase B/C — `translation/tests/differential.rs`\n23 test functions driving **~140 distinct stdin inputs** through *both* binaries as subprocesses, asserting stdout, stderr **and** exit status (code *and* terminating signal) byte-for-byte. No test is ignored or skipped. The C binary is built by the harness if missing.\n\n## Mismatches found and fixed (all in `translation/ERRORS.md`)\n\nThe C keeps *using* nodes after `free()`, so its observable output exposes glibc's allocator. Six real divergences:\n\n| # | Input | C | Rust before |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.1 | `1 A / 7 A / 4 A` | `City 'A' not found` | found it — `free()` overwrites the first 16 bytes of `city_name` |\n| 1.1b | 7 frees, then `7 N10 / 4 N10` | **finds** N10 | not found — a chunk absorbed into the top chunk keeps its name |\n| 1.2 | `1 A / 7 A / 1 A / 3` | adds it again, into the *same chunk* | \"already exists\" |\n| 1.2b | 12 cities, `7 N9 / 7 N11`, 9 adds | reuse is newest-freed-first | ascending address order |\n| 1.3 | `…/ 7 B / 6 A / 8` | SIGABRT 134, empty stdout, 3 distinct glibc messages | exit 0 |\n| 1.5 | 4 cities + a `2147483647` back-edge | **exit 0, prints a 101-entry path** | SIGSEGV |\n\n1.5 was caught by an independent audit sub-agent and I reproduced it myself: the `path[MAX_NODES]` overrun lands in the `state` array (`&path[100] == &state[0]`), which usually *stops* the loop rather than crashing. Modelling that word-by-word aliasing makes all 29 shapes of a `k`-city-chain sweep match, including the odd 101/107/110/111 entry counts and the four that do crash.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n- Both build with no errors; `cargo test` passes 23/23 in debug and release, 8 consecutive runs, no flakiness.\n- ~2000 randomized differential sessions (general, delete-heavy, overflow-biased) found no further divergence. Harnesses kept in `translation/tools/`.\n- `c_src/` is untouched (sources still carry their original timestamps; only `build/` was created).\n- Two input classes are inherently irreproducible and are documented rather than hidden: the bytes a freed node's name prints as (an ASLR-dependent heap address — the C disagrees with *itself* run to run) and which signal a genuinely smashed frame raises (SIGSEGV ~80% / SIGBUS ~20%). Both have tests that still assert everything deterministic around them."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1050,"cache_read_input_tokens":325744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1050,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c291cb1f-2668-45aa-801e-ac393f2cc752","uuid":"c4a000ec-ebf0-4e95-a4b6-9093879b8652","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:24:46.519Z"}
